{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengzheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Mengzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfei","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Yanfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Osei","familyName":"Opoku","name":"Opoku, David Osei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruochang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruochang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunxiao","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Chunxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DSRAG: A Domain-Specific Retrieval Framework Based on Document-derived Multimodal Knowledge Graph"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-22T14:24:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:00:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current general-purpose large language models (LLMs) commonly exhibit knowledge hallucination and insufficient domain-specific adaptability in domain-specific tasks, limiting their effectiveness in specialized question answering scenarios. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) effectively tackles these challenges by integrating external knowledge to enhance accuracy and relevance. However, traditional RAG still faces limitations in domain knowledge accuracy and context modeling.To enhance domain-specific question answering performance, this work focuses on a graph-based RAG framework, emphasizing the critical role of knowledge graph quality during the generation process. We propose DSRAG (Domain-Specific RAG), a multimodal knowledge graph-driven retrieval-augmented generation framework designed for domain-specific applications. Our approach leverages domain-specific documents as the primary knowledge source, integrating heterogeneous information such as text, images, and tables to construct a multimodal knowledge graph covering both conceptual and instance layers. Building on this foundation, we introduce semantic pruning and structured subgraph retrieval mechanisms, combining knowledge graph context and vector retrieval results to guide the language model towards producing more reliable responses. Evaluations using the Langfuse multidimensional scoring mechanism show that our method excels in domain-specific question answering, validating the efficacy of integrating multimodal knowledge graphs with retrieval-augmented generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 5 figures. Accepted to the 22nd International Conference on Web Information Systems and Applications (WISA 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:39:56Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:39:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:39:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10469","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10469","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10469","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesse","familyName":"Ponnock","name":"Ponnock, Jesse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grace","familyName":"Kenneally","name":"Kenneally, Grace","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael Robert","familyName":"Briggs","name":"Briggs, Michael Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elinor","familyName":"Yeo","name":"Yeo, Elinor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyrone","familyName":"Patterson","name":"Patterson, Tyrone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Kinberg","name":"Kinberg, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Kalinowski","name":"Kalinowski, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Hechtman","name":"Hechtman, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Real-Time RAG for the Identification of Supply Chain Vulnerabilities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; H.3.3; I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-23T22:06:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T02:43:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"New technologies in generative AI can enable deeper analysis into our nation's supply chains but truly informative insights require the continual updating and aggregation of massive data in a timely manner. Large Language Models (LLMs) offer unprecedented analytical opportunities however, their knowledge base is constrained to the models' last training date, rendering these capabilities unusable for organizations whose mission impacts rely on emerging and timely information. This research proposes an innovative approach to supply chain analysis by integrating emerging Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) preprocessing and retrieval techniques with advanced web-scraping technologies. Our method aims to reduce latency in incorporating new information into an augmented-LLM, enabling timely analysis of supply chain disruptors. Through experimentation, this study evaluates the combinatorial effects of these techniques towards timeliness and quality trade-offs. Our results suggest that in applying RAG systems to supply chain analysis, fine-tuning the embedding retrieval model consistently provides the most significant performance gains, underscoring the critical importance of retrieval quality. Adaptive iterative retrieval, which dynamically adjusts retrieval depth based on context, further enhances performance, especially on complex supply chain queries. Conversely, fine-tuning the LLM yields limited improvements and higher resource costs, while techniques such as downward query abstraction significantly outperforms upward abstraction in practice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 5 figures, 1 table. Approved for Public Release; Distribution Unlimited. PRS Release Number: 25-0864"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10469","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:39:59Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:39:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:41:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10478","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10478","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10478","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oluwaseyi","familyName":"Giwa","name":"Giwa, Oluwaseyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Adewole","name":"Adewole, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobi","familyName":"Awodumila","name":"Awodumila, Tobi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pelumi","familyName":"Aderinto","name":"Aderinto, Pelumi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The LLM as a Network Operator: A Vision for Generative AI in the 6G Radio Access Network"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-27T20:47:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:00:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The management of future AI-native Next-Generation (NextG) Radio Access Networks (RANs), including 6G and beyond, presents a challenge of immense complexity that exceeds the capabilities of traditional automation. In response, we introduce the concept of the LLM-RAN Operator. In this paradigm, a Large Language Model (LLM) is embedded into the RAN control loop to translate high-level human intents into optimal network actions. Unlike prior empirical studies, we present a formal framework for an LLM-RAN operator that builds on earlier work by making guarantees checkable through an adapter aligned with the Open RAN (O-RAN) standard, separating strategic LLM-driven guidance in the Non-Real-Time (RT) RAN intelligent controller (RIC) from reactive execution in the Near-RT RIC, including a proposition on policy expressiveness and a theorem on convergence to stable fixed points. By framing the problem with mathematical rigor, our work provides the analytical tools to reason about the feasibility and stability of AI-native RAN control. It identifies critical research challenges in safety, real-time performance, and physical-world grounding. This paper aims to bridge the gap between AI theory and wireless systems engineering in the NextG era, aligning with the AI4NextG vision to develop knowledgeable, intent-driven wireless networks that integrate generative AI into the heart of the RAN."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to Workshop on AI and ML for Next-Generation Wireless Communications and Networking, NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10478","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:40:12Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:40:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:40:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10509","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10509","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10509","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai Teja Reddy","familyName":"Adapala","name":"Adapala, Sai Teja Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Anti-Ouroboros Effect: Emergent Resilience in Large Language Models from Recursive Selective Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-02T05:46:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:01:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The stability of recursively trained large language models (LLMs) is a foundational problem for AI safety. Prevailing theory predicts model collapse, a progressive degradation when models are trained on their own output. We challenge this narrative by introducing a selective feedback mechanism. Contrary to expectation, instead of merely slowing decay, our experiments provide strong evidence that this pressure reverses it, inducing a statistically significant performance improvement in a Gemma 2B model on a complex summarization task. We name this phenomenon the Anti-Ouroboros Effect. We contrast this with a foundational experiment using a simple classifier, where the theoretical degenerative loop was validated, highlighting the unique dynamics of high-dimensional models. Our findings establish that systemic resilience can be an emergent property of LLMs under simple selection pressure, suggesting a powerful and scalable principle for developing safer and more robust AI systems. Across five generations, a quality-filtered condition improved by 6.6% in ROUGE-L F1 score, whereas an unfiltered control degraded by 3.5% and a random-filter control degraded by 4.2%"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 3 figures, 2 tables. Code is available at: https://github.com/imsaitejareddy/ouroboros-effect-experiment"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10509","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:40:55Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:40:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:40:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10518","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10518","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10518","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justin","familyName":"Arndt","name":"Arndt, Justin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Holographic Knowledge Manifolds: A Novel Pipeline for Continual Learning Without Catastrophic Forgetting in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T16:54:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:01:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-21T00:59:21Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; This paper includes significant errors discovered post publication by the author"},{"date":"2025-11-24T01:11:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce the Holographic Knowledge Manifold (HKM), a four-phase pipeline that achieves zero catastrophic forgetting in AI knowledge representation while maintaining minimal memory growth and high efficiency. Leveraging fractal quantization, probabilistic entanglement, and dynamic diffraction chipping, HKM compresses knowledge substrates by 3x with 67% storage savings, integrates holographically at 100%, and supports over 1,020 updates with 1% growth per increment. In experiments on combined WikiText and FB15k datasets (scaled to 2,997 nodes), we demonstrate industry-leading performance: 0% forgetting (infinite improvement over GEM baselines), 3x compression, and 53% training time reduction on consumer GPU hardware. Hypothetical cost analyses project $92.4M savings over 5 years at petabyte scale, with 21.2% energy reduction and 33% lower carbon footprint. This work hypothesizes a paradigm shift for public large language models (LLMs), enabling \"eternal\" adaptation without retraining. Future extensions to multimodal fusion and quantum hardware could further democratize scalable AI, potentially reducing fine-tuning costs by 60-80% for models like Llama-3 or Grok-4. Code, datasets, and full results are publicly available for reproducibility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper includes significant errors discovered post publication by the author"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10518","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:41:09Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:41:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-24T02:48:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10570","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10570","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10570","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shengen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sisuo","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Sisuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haicheng","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Haicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Limin","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Limin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiping","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Weiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runwei","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Runwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutao","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Yutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Foundation Models for Trajectory Prediction in Autonomous Driving: A Comprehensive Survey"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T10:30:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:02:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Trajectory prediction serves as a critical functionality in autonomous driving, enabling the anticipation of future motion paths for traffic participants such as vehicles and pedestrians, which is essential for driving safety. Although conventional deep learning methods have improved accuracy, they remain hindered by inherent limitations, including lack of interpretability, heavy reliance on large-scale annotated data, and weak generalization in long-tail scenarios. The rise of Large Foundation Models (LFMs) is transforming the research paradigm of trajectory prediction. This survey offers a systematic review of recent advances in LFMs, particularly Large Language Models (LLMs) and Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) for trajectory prediction. By integrating linguistic and scene semantics, LFMs facilitate interpretable contextual reasoning, significantly enhancing prediction safety and generalization in complex environments. The article highlights three core methodologies: trajectory-language mapping, multimodal fusion, and constraint-based reasoning. It covers prediction tasks for both vehicles and pedestrians, evaluation metrics, and dataset analyses. Key challenges such as computational latency, data scarcity, and real-world robustness are discussed, along with future research directions including low-latency inference, causality-aware modeling, and motion foundation models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10570","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:42:22Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:42:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:42:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10572","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10572","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10572","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashlesha","familyName":"Akella","name":"Akella, Ashlesha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshar","familyName":"Kaul","name":"Kaul, Akshar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krishnasuri","familyName":"Narayanam","name":"Narayanam, Krishnasuri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sameep","familyName":"Mehta","name":"Mehta, Sameep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quality Assessment of Tabular Data using Large Language Models and Code Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T14:17:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:02:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T02:54:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:44:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reliable data quality is crucial for downstream analysis of tabular datasets, yet rule-based validation often struggles with inefficiency, human intervention, and high computational costs. We present a three-stage framework that combines statistical inliner detection with LLM-driven rule and code generation. After filtering data samples through traditional clustering, we iteratively prompt LLMs to produce semantically valid quality rules and synthesize their executable validators through code-generating LLMs. To generate reliable quality rules, we aid LLMs with retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) by leveraging external knowledge sources and domain-specific few-shot examples. Robust guardrails ensure the accuracy and consistency of both rules and code snippets. Extensive evaluations on benchmark datasets confirm the effectiveness of our approach."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10572","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:42:24Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:42:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:50:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10575","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10575","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10575","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiao","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Qiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyong","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhiyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gene-R1: Reasoning with Data-Augmented Lightweight LLMs for Gene Set Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Genomics (q-bio.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:14:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:02:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The gene set analysis (GSA) is a foundational approach for uncovering the molecular functions associated with a group of genes. Recently, LLM-powered methods have emerged to annotate gene sets with biological functions together with coherent explanatory insights. However, existing studies primarily focus on proprietary models, which have been shown to outperform their open-source counterparts despite concerns over cost and data privacy. Furthermore, no research has investigated the application of advanced reasoning strategies to the GSA task. To address this gap, we introduce Gene-R1, a data-augmented learning framework that equips lightweight and open-source LLMs with step-by-step reasoning capabilities tailored to GSA. Experiments on 1,508 in-distribution gene sets demonstrate that Gene-R1 achieves substantial performance gains, matching commercial LLMs. On 106 out-of-distribution gene sets, Gene-R1 performs comparably to both commercial and large-scale LLMs, exhibiting robust generalizability across diverse gene sources."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 4 figures, 6 tables, 40 references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10575","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:42:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:42:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:42:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10584","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10584","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10584","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiaofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zisu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zisu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Janice","familyName":"Krieger","name":"Krieger, Janice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohan","familyName":"Zalake","name":"Zalake, Mohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Smart Trial: Evaluating the Use of Large Language Models for Recruiting Clinical Trial Participants via Social Media"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T01:34:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:03:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Clinical trials (CT) are essential for advancing medical research and treatment, yet efficiently recruiting eligible participants -- each of whom must meet complex eligibility criteria -- remains a significant challenge. Traditional recruitment approaches, such as advertisements or electronic health record screening within hospitals, are often time-consuming and geographically constrained. This work addresses the recruitment challenge by leveraging the vast amount of health-related information individuals share on social media platforms. With the emergence of powerful large language models (LLMs) capable of sophisticated text understanding, we pose the central research question: Can LLM-driven tools facilitate CT recruitment by identifying potential participants through their engagement on social media? To investigate this question, we introduce TRIALQA, a novel dataset comprising two social media collections from the subreddits on colon cancer and prostate cancer. Using eligibility criteria from public real-world CTs, experienced annotators are hired to annotate TRIALQA to indicate (1) whether a social media user meets a given eligibility criterion and (2) the user's stated reasons for interest in participating in CT. We benchmark seven widely used LLMs on these two prediction tasks, employing six distinct training and inference strategies. Our extensive experiments reveal that, while LLMs show considerable promise, they still face challenges in performing the complex, multi-hop reasoning needed to accurately assess eligibility criteria."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10584","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:42:41Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:42:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:42:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10625","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10625","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10625","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iván Vicente Moreno","familyName":"Cencerrado","name":"Cencerrado, Iván Vicente Moreno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnau Padrés","familyName":"Masdemont","name":"Masdemont, Arnau Padrés","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton Gonzalvez","familyName":"Hawthorne","name":"Hawthorne, Anton Gonzalvez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Demitri","familyName":"Africa","name":"Africa, David Demitri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Pacchiardi","name":"Pacchiardi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"No Answer Needed: Predicting LLM Answer Accuracy from Question-Only Linear Probes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T18:09:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:04:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-09T12:15:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:48:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T10:11:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:41:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Do large language models (LLMs) anticipate when they will answer correctly? To study this, we extract activations after a question is read but before any tokens are generated, and train linear probes to predict whether the model's forthcoming answer will be correct. Across three open-source model families ranging from 7 to 70 billion parameters, projections on this \"in-advance correctness direction\" trained on generic trivia questions predict success in distribution and on diverse out-of-distribution knowledge datasets, indicating a deeper signal than dataset-specific spurious features, and outperforming black-box baselines and verbalised predicted confidence. Predictive power saturates in intermediate layers and, notably, generalisation falters on questions requiring mathematical reasoning. Moreover, for models responding \"I don't know\", doing so strongly correlates with the probe score, indicating that the same direction also captures confidence. By complementing previous results on truthfulness and other behaviours obtained with probes and sparse auto-encoders, our work contributes essential findings to elucidate LLM internals."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted (poster) to Principled Design for Trustworthy AI at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10625","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:43:38Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:43:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:17:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Rajaneesh","name":"Rajaneesh, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Zollo","name":"Zollo, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Zemel","name":"Zemel, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Warmup for Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T18:58:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:05:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-06T12:24:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-07T01:39:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) hold great promise for advanced reasoning at the intersection of text and images, yet they have not fully realized this potential. MLLMs typically integrate an LLM, a vision encoder, and a connector that maps the vision encoder's embeddings into the LLM's text embedding space. Although each component is pretrained on massive datasets with billions of samples, the entire multimodal model is typically trained on only thousands (or a few million) samples, which can result in weak performance on complex reasoning tasks. To address these shortcomings, instead of relying on extensive labeled datasets for fine-tuning, we propose a Test-Time Warmup method that adapts the MLLM per test instance by leveraging data from weakly supervised auxiliary tasks. With our approach, we observe a relative performance improvement of 4.03% on MMMU, 5.28% on VQA-Rad, and 1.63% on GQA on the Llama-Vision-Instruct model. Our method demonstrates that 'warming up' before inference can enhance MLLMs' robustness across diverse reasoning tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:00Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-07T03:05:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10655","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10655","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10655","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charankumar","familyName":"Akiri","name":"Akiri, Charankumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harrison","familyName":"Simpson","name":"Simpson, Harrison","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kshitiz","familyName":"Aryal","name":"Aryal, Kshitiz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aarav","familyName":"Khanna","name":"Khanna, Aarav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maanak","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Maanak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Benchmarking Risk Profile and Harm Potential"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T19:34:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:06:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T02:00:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:22:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While the widespread deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) holds great potential for society, their vulnerabilities to adversarial manipulation and exploitation can pose serious safety, security, and ethical risks. As new threats continue to emerge, it becomes critically necessary to assess the landscape of LLMs' safety and security against evolving adversarial prompt techniques. To understand the behavior of LLMs, this research provides an empirical analysis and risk profile of nine prominent LLMs, Claude Opus 4, DeepSeek V3 (both open-source and online), Gemini 2.5 Flash, GPT-4o, Grok 3, Llama 4 Scout, Mistral 7B, and Qwen 3 1.7B, against 24 different security and safety categories. These LLMs are evaluated on their ability to produce harmful responses for adversarially crafted prompts (dataset has been made public) for a broad range of safety and security topics, such as promotion of violent criminal behavior, promotion of non-violent criminal activity, societal harms related to safety, illegal sexual content, dangerous code generation, and cybersecurity threats beyond code. Our study introduces the Risk Severity Index (RSI), an agile and scalable evaluation score, to quantify and compare the security posture and creating a risk profile of LLMs. As the LLM development landscape progresses, the RSI is intended to be a valuable metric for comparing the risks of LLMs across evolving threats. This research finds widespread vulnerabilities in the safety filters of the LLMs tested and highlights the urgent need for stronger alignment, responsible deployment practices, and model governance, particularly for open-access and rapidly iterated models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10655","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:20Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-29T02:52:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10697","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10697","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10697","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengcheng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Pengcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siru","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Siru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhu","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Yizhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runchu","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Runchu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey on Retrieval And Structuring Augmented Generation with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:25:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:09:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3711896.3736557","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized natural language processing with their remarkable capabilities in text generation and reasoning. However, these models face critical challenges when deployed in real-world applications, including hallucination generation, outdated knowledge, and limited domain expertise. Retrieval And Structuring (RAS) Augmented Generation addresses these limitations by integrating dynamic information retrieval with structured knowledge representations. This survey (1) examines retrieval mechanisms including sparse, dense, and hybrid approaches for accessing external knowledge; (2) explore text structuring techniques such as taxonomy construction, hierarchical classification, and information extraction that transform unstructured text into organized representations; and (3) investigate how these structured representations integrate with LLMs through prompt-based methods, reasoning frameworks, and knowledge embedding techniques. It also identifies technical challenges in retrieval efficiency, structure quality, and knowledge integration, while highlighting research opportunities in multimodal retrieval, cross-lingual structures, and interactive systems. This comprehensive overview provides researchers and practitioners with insights into RAS methods, applications, and future directions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"KDD'25 survey track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10697","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:19Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10698","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10698","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10698","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rabeya Tus","familyName":"Sadia","name":"Sadia, Rabeya Tus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CrunchLLM: Multitask LLMs for Structured Business Reasoning and Outcome Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:26:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:09:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T21:54:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:47:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predicting the success of start-up companies, defined as achieving an exit through acquisition or IPO, is a critical problem in entrepreneurship and innovation research. Datasets such as Crunchbase provide both structured information (e.g., funding rounds, industries, investor networks) and unstructured text (e.g., company descriptions), but effectively leveraging this heterogeneous data for prediction remains challenging. Traditional machine learning approaches often rely only on structured features and achieve moderate accuracy, while large language models (LLMs) offer rich reasoning abilities but struggle to adapt directly to domain-specific business data. We present \\textbf{CrunchLLM}, a domain-adapted LLM framework for startup success prediction. CrunchLLM integrates structured company attributes with unstructured textual narratives and applies parameter-efficient fine-tuning strategies alongside prompt optimization to specialize foundation models for entrepreneurship data. Our approach achieves accuracy exceeding 80\\% on Crunchbase startup success prediction, significantly outperforming traditional classifiers and baseline LLMs. Beyond predictive performance, CrunchLLM provides interpretable reasoning traces that justify its predictions, enhancing transparency and trustworthiness for financial and policy decision makers. This work demonstrates how adapting LLMs with domain-aware fine-tuning and structured--unstructured data fusion can advance predictive modeling of entrepreneurial outcomes. CrunchLLM contributes a methodological framework and a practical tool for data-driven decision making in venture capital and innovation policy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10698","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:20Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:20:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10704","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10704","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10704","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingchen","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Xingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ruoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hootan","familyName":"Nakhost","name":"Nakhost, Hootan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajarishi","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Rajarishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sercan Ö.","familyName":"Arık","name":"Arık, Sercan Ö.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:45:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:10:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image (T2I) models, while offering immense creative potential, are highly reliant on human intervention, posing significant usability challenges that often necessitate manual, iterative prompt engineering over often underspecified prompts. This paper introduces Maestro, a novel self-evolving image generation system that enables T2I models to autonomously self-improve generated images through iterative evolution of prompts, using only an initial prompt. Maestro incorporates two key innovations: 1) self-critique, where specialized multimodal LLM (MLLM) agents act as 'critics' to identify weaknesses in generated images, correct for under-specification, and provide interpretable edit signals, which are then integrated by a 'verifier' agent while preserving user intent; and 2) self-evolution, utilizing MLLM-as-a-judge for head-to-head comparisons between iteratively generated images, eschewing problematic images, and evolving creative prompt candidates that align with user intents. Extensive experiments on complex T2I tasks using black-box models demonstrate that Maestro significantly improves image quality over initial prompts and state-of-the-art automated methods, with effectiveness scaling with more advanced MLLM components. This work presents a robust, interpretable, and effective pathway towards self-improving T2I generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 7 figures, 2 tables (22 pages, 9 figures and 3 tables including references and appendices)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10704","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:29Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10708","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10708","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10708","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iman","familyName":"Barati","name":"Barati, Iman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mostafa","familyName":"Amiri","name":"Amiri, Mostafa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heshaam","familyName":"Faili","name":"Faili, Heshaam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SearchInstruct: Enhancing Domain Adaptation via Retrieval-Based Instruction Dataset Creation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:50:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:10:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) is essential for training large language models (LLMs), significantly enhancing critical capabilities such as instruction following and in-context learning. Nevertheless, creating suitable training datasets tailored for specific domains remains challenging due to unique domain constraints and data scarcity. In this paper, we propose SearchInstruct, an innovative method explicitly designed to construct high quality instruction datasets for SFT. Our approach begins with a limited set of domain specific, human generated questions, which are systematically expanded using a large language model. Subsequently, domain relevant resources are dynamically retrieved to generate accurate and contextually appropriate answers for each augmented question. Experimental evaluation demonstrates that SearchInstruct enhances both the diversity and quality of SFT datasets, leading to measurable improvements in LLM performance within specialized domains. Additionally, we show that beyond dataset generation, the proposed method can also effectively facilitate tasks such as model editing, enabling efficient updates to existing models. To facilitate reproducibility and community adoption, we provide full implementation details, the complete set of generated instruction response pairs, and the source code in a publicly accessible Git repository: [https://github.com/mostafaamiri/SearchInstruct](https://github.com/mostafaamiri/SearchInstruct)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10708","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:35Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10723","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10723","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10723","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoran","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chaoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingcan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Bingcan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Khalilov","name":"Khalilov, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simret Araya","familyName":"Gebreegziabher","name":"Gebreegziabher, Simret Araya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingsheng","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Bingsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dakuo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dakuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yanfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxing","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toby Jia-Jun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Toby Jia-Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dark Patterns Meet GUI Agents: LLM Agent Susceptibility to Manipulative Interfaces and the Role of Human Oversight"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T22:26:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:11:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The dark patterns, deceptive interface designs manipulating user behaviors, have been extensively studied for their effects on human decision-making and autonomy. Yet, with the rising prominence of LLM-powered GUI agents that automate tasks from high-level intents, understanding how dark patterns affect agents is increasingly important. We present a two-phase empirical study examining how agents, human participants, and human-AI teams respond to 16 types of dark patterns across diverse scenarios. Phase 1 highlights that agents often fail to recognize dark patterns, and even when aware, prioritize task completion over protective action. Phase 2 revealed divergent failure modes: humans succumb due to cognitive shortcuts and habitual compliance, while agents falter from procedural blind spots. Human oversight improved avoidance but introduced costs such as attentional tunneling and cognitive load. Our findings show neither humans nor agents are uniformly resilient, and collaboration introduces new vulnerabilities, suggesting design needs for transparency, adjustable autonomy, and oversight."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10723","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:56Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10729","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10729","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10729","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilker","familyName":"Demirel","name":"Demirel, Ilker","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karan","familyName":"Thakkar","name":"Thakkar, Karan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Elizalde","name":"Elizalde, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miquel Espi","familyName":"Marques","name":"Marques, Miquel Espi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Sarathy","name":"Sarathy, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umamahesh","familyName":"Srinivas","name":"Srinivas, Umamahesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shirley","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Shirley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaya","familyName":"Narain","name":"Narain, Jaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Using LLMs for Late Multimodal Sensor Fusion for Activity Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T22:36:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:11:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-23T01:46:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T01:44:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-19T23:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-23T01:10:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sensor data streams provide valuable information around activities and context for downstream applications, though integrating complementary information can be challenging. We show that large language models (LLMs) can be used for late fusion for activity classification from audio and motion time series data. We curated a subset of data for diverse activity recognition across contexts (e.g., household activities, sports) from the Ego4D dataset. Evaluated LLMs achieved 12-class zero- and one-shot classification F1-scores significantly above chance, with no task-specific training. Zero-shot classification via LLM-based fusion from modality-specific models can enable multimodal temporal applications where there is limited aligned training data for learning a shared embedding space. Additionally, LLM-based fusion can enable model deploying without requiring additional memory and computation for targeted application-specific multimodal models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS Workshop on Learning from Time Series for Health"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10729","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:04Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-23T04:23:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10739","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10739","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10739","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mobina","familyName":"Pournemat","name":"Pournemat, Mobina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keivan","familyName":"Rezaei","name":"Rezaei, Keivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaurang","familyName":"Sriramanan","name":"Sriramanan, Gaurang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Zarei","name":"Zarei, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Eghbalzadeh","name":"Eghbalzadeh, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soheil","familyName":"Feizi","name":"Feizi, Soheil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T22:58:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:11:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T15:28:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T01:10:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite widespread success in language understanding and generation, large language models (LLMs) exhibit unclear and often inconsistent behavior when faced with tasks that require probabilistic reasoning. In this work, we present the first comprehensive study of the reasoning capabilities of LLMs over explicit discrete probability distributions. Given observations from a probability distribution, we evaluate models on three carefully designed tasks, mode identification, maximum likelihood estimation, and sample generation, by prompting them to provide responses to queries about either the joint distribution or its conditionals. These tasks thus probe a range of probabilistic skills, including frequency analysis, marginalization, and generative behavior. Through comprehensive empirical evaluations, we demonstrate that there exists a clear performance gap between smaller and larger models, with the latter demonstrating stronger inference and surprising capabilities in sample generation. Furthermore, our investigations reveal notable limitations, including sensitivity to variations in the notation utilized to represent probabilistic outcomes and performance degradation of over 60% as context length increases. Together, our results provide a detailed understanding of the probabilistic reasoning abilities of LLMs and identify key directions for future improvement."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10739","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:18Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-29T02:56:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10744","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10744","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10744","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ozan","familyName":"Gokdemir","name":"Gokdemir, Ozan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neil","familyName":"Getty","name":"Getty, Neil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Underwood","name":"Underwood, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandeep","familyName":"Madireddy","name":"Madireddy, Sandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franck","familyName":"Cappello","name":"Cappello, Franck","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arvind","familyName":"Ramanathan","name":"Ramanathan, Arvind","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ian T.","familyName":"Foster","name":"Foster, Ian T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rick L.","familyName":"Stevens","name":"Stevens, Rick L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated MCQA Benchmarking at Scale: Evaluating Reasoning Traces as Retrieval Sources for Domain Adaptation of Small Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T23:22:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:12:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As scientific knowledge grows at an unprecedented pace, evaluation benchmarks must evolve to reflect new discoveries and ensure language models are tested on current, diverse literature. We propose a scalable, modular framework for generating multiple-choice question-answering (MCQA) benchmarks directly from large corpora of scientific papers. Our pipeline automates every stage of MCQA creation, including PDF parsing, semantic chunking, question generation, and model evaluation. As a case study, we generate more than 16,000 MCQs from 22,000 open-access articles in radiation and cancer biology. We then evaluate a suite of small language models (1.1B-14B parameters) on these questions, comparing baseline accuracy with retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) from paper-derived semantic chunks and from reasoning traces distilled from GPT-4.1. We find that reasoning-trace retrieval consistently improves performance on both synthetic and expert-annotated benchmarks, enabling several small models to surpass GPT-4 on the 2023 Astro Radiation and Cancer Biology exam."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This manuscript has been accepted for publication at the Supercomputing 25 (SC '25) Conference (Frontiers in Generative AI for HPC Science and Engineering: Foundations, Challenges, and Opportunities Workshop) in St. Louis, MO, USA on November 16th, 2025. It will appear in the SC25 Workshop Proceedings after that date"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10744","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:25Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:46:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10746","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10746","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10746","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adarsh","familyName":"Srinivasan","name":"Srinivasan, Adarsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Dineen","name":"Dineen, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Umar","familyName":"Afzal","name":"Afzal, Muhammad Umar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Uzair","familyName":"Sarfraz","name":"Sarfraz, Muhammad Uzair","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irbaz B.","familyName":"Riaz","name":"Riaz, Irbaz B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RECAP: Transparent Inference-Time Emotion Alignment for Medical Dialogue Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T23:30:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:12:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-03T02:09:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:16:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-03T17:31:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-05T01:08:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models in healthcare often produce emotionally flat or opaque responses, failing to provide the transparent reasoning required for clinical trust. We present RECAP (Reflect-Extract-Calibrate-Align-Produce), an inference-time framework grounded in cognitive appraisal theory that decomposes patient input into auditable, appraisal-theoretic stages without retraining. Across multiple benchmarks and models from 8B to 120B parameters, RECAP improves alignment with human judgments, with gains inversely proportional to model scale. Intermediate outputs further reveal that models systematically underweight relational factors such as social support. In blinded evaluations, oncology fellows rated RECAP responses significantly higher than baselines with 76-88% win rates, demonstrating that principled prompting can enhance medical AI's emotional intelligence while maintaining the transparency required for clinical deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"LREC 2026: ClinicalNLP Workshop (Oral)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10746","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T04:00:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10748","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10748","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10748","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jecia Z. Y.","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Jecia Z. Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francis X","familyName":"Creighton","name":"Creighton, Francis X","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Russell H","familyName":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, Russell H","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manish","familyName":"Sahu","name":"Sahu, Manish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SCOPE: Speech-guided COllaborative PErception Framework for Surgical Scene Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T23:36:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:12:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate segmentation and tracking of relevant elements of the surgical scene is crucial to enable context-aware intraoperative assistance and decision making. Current solutions remain tethered to domain-specific, supervised models that rely on labeled data and required domain-specific data to adapt to new surgical scenarios and beyond predefined label categories. Recent advances in prompt-driven vision foundation models (VFM) have enabled open-set, zero-shot segmentation across heterogeneous medical images. However, dependence of these models on manual visual or textual cues restricts their deployment in introperative surgical settings. We introduce a speech-guided collaborative perception (SCOPE) framework that integrates reasoning capabilities of large language model (LLM) with perception capabilities of open-set VFMs to support on-the-fly segmentation, labeling and tracking of surgical instruments and anatomy in intraoperative video streams. A key component of this framework is a collaborative perception agent, which generates top candidates of VFM-generated segmentation and incorporates intuitive speech feedback from clinicians to guide the segmentation of surgical instruments in a natural human-machine collaboration paradigm. Afterwards, instruments themselves serve as interactive pointers to label additional elements of the surgical scene. We evaluated our proposed framework on a subset of publicly available Cataract1k dataset and an in-house ex-vivo skull-base dataset to demonstrate its potential to generate on-the-fly segmentation and tracking of surgical scene. Furthermore, we demonstrate its dynamic capabilities through a live mock ex-vivo experiment. This human-AI collaboration paradigm showcase the potential of developing adaptable, hands-free, surgeon-centric tools for dynamic operating-room environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10748","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:46:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10753","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10753","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10753","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Minh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian K.","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Brian K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syed A.","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Syed A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geigh","familyName":"Zollicoffer","name":"Zollicoffer, Geigh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nhat","familyName":"Hoang-Xuan","name":"Hoang-Xuan, Nhat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manish","familyName":"Bhattarai","name":"Bhattarai, Manish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T23:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:12:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) exhibit impressive reasoning and question-answering capabilities. However, they often produce inaccurate or unreliable content known as hallucinations. This unreliability significantly limits their deployment in high-stakes applications. Thus, there is a growing need for a general-purpose method to detect hallucinations in LLMs. In this work, we introduce HalluField, a novel field-theoretic approach for hallucination detection based on a parametrized variational principle and thermodynamics. Inspired by thermodynamics, HalluField models an LLM's response to a given query and temperature setting as a collection of discrete likelihood token paths, each associated with a corresponding energy and entropy. By analyzing how energy and entropy distributions vary across token paths under changes in temperature and likelihood, HalluField quantifies the semantic stability of a response. Hallucinations are then detected by identifying unstable or erratic behavior in this energy landscape. HalluField is computationally efficient and highly practical: it operates directly on the model's output logits without requiring fine-tuning or auxiliary neural networks. Notably, the method is grounded in a principled physical interpretation, drawing analogies to the first law of thermodynamics. Remarkably, by modeling LLM behavior through this physical lens, HalluField achieves state-of-the-art hallucination detection performance across models and datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10753","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:38Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:46:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10761","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10761","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10761","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcelo","familyName":"Sandoval-Castaneda","name":"Sandoval-Castaneda, Marcelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Russell","name":"Russell, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josef","familyName":"Sivic","name":"Sivic, Josef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregory","familyName":"Shakhnarovich","name":"Shakhnarovich, Gregory","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian Caba","familyName":"Heilbron","name":"Heilbron, Fabian Caba","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EditDuet: A Multi-Agent System for Video Non-Linear Editing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T00:27:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:26:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3721238.3730761","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated tools for video editing and assembly have applications ranging from filmmaking and advertisement to content creation for social media. Previous video editing work has mainly focused on either retrieval or user interfaces, leaving actual editing to the user. In contrast, we propose to automate the core task of video editing, formulating it as sequential decision making process. Ours is a multi-agent approach. We design an Editor agent and a Critic agent. The Editor takes as input a collection of video clips together with natural language instructions and uses tools commonly found in video editing software to produce an edited sequence. On the other hand, the Critic gives natural language feedback to the editor based on the produced sequence or renders it if it is satisfactory. We introduce a learning-based approach for enabling effective communication across specialized agents to address the language-driven video editing task. Finally, we explore an LLM-as-a-judge metric for evaluating the quality of video editing system and compare it with general human preference. We evaluate our system's output video sequences qualitatively and quantitatively through a user study and find that our system vastly outperforms existing approaches in terms of coverage, time constraint satisfaction, and human preference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"SIGGRAPH 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10761","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:49Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-30T02:19:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10769","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10769","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10769","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tara","familyName":"Bogavelli","name":"Bogavelli, Tara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roshnee","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Roshnee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hari","familyName":"Subramani","name":"Subramani, Hari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentArch: A Comprehensive Benchmark to Evaluate Agent Architectures in Enterprise"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T01:18:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:13:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-06T18:18:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:56:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While individual components of agentic architectures have been studied in isolation, there remains limited empirical understanding of how different design dimensions interact within complex multi-agent systems. This study aims to address these gaps by providing a comprehensive enterprise-specific benchmark evaluating 18 distinct agentic configurations across state-of-the-art large language models. We examine four critical agentic system dimensions: orchestration strategy, agent prompt implementation (ReAct versus function calling), memory architecture, and thinking tool integration. Our benchmark reveals significant model-specific architectural preferences that challenge the prevalent one-size-fits-all paradigm in agentic AI systems. It also reveals significant weaknesses in overall agentic performance on enterprise tasks with the highest scoring models achieving a maximum of only 35.3\\% success on the more complex task and 70.8\\% on the simpler task. We hope these findings inform the design of future agentic systems by enabling more empirically backed decisions regarding architectural components and model selection."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10769","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:47:01Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:47:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-07T02:59:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10798","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10798","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10798","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhuang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuzhuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingfu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Qingfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanxiang","familyName":"Che","name":"Che, Wanxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Judge Q: Trainable Queries for Optimized Information Retention in KV Cache Eviction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T03:34:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:16:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-15T09:11:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-16T01:31:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) utilize key-value (KV) cache to store historical information during sequence processing. The size of KV cache grows linearly as the length of the sequence extends, which seriously affects memory usage and decoding efficiency. Current methods for KV cache eviction typically utilize the last window from the pre-filling phase as queries to compute the KV importance scores for eviction. Although this scheme is simple to implement, it tends to overly focus on local information, potentially leading to the neglect or omission of crucial global information. To mitigate this issue, we propose Judge Q, a novel training method which incorporates a soft token list. This method only tunes the model's embedding layer at a low training cost. By concatenating the soft token list at the end of the input sequence, we train these tokens' attention map to the original input sequence to align with that of the actual decoded tokens. In this way, the queries corresponding to the soft tokens can effectively capture global information and better evaluate the importance of the keys and values within the KV cache, thus maintaining decoding quality when KV cache is evicted. Under the same eviction budget, our method exhibits less performance degradation compared to existing eviction approaches. We validate our approach through experiments conducted on models such as Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3, using benchmarks including LongBench, RULER, and Needle-in-a-Haystack. Results indicate an improvement of approximately 1 point on the LongBench and over 3 points on RULER. This proposed methodology can be seamlessly integrated into existing open-source models with minimal training overhead, thereby enhancing performance in KV cache eviction scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in AAAI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10798","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:47:41Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:47:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-16T03:10:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10818","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10818","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10818","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris","familyName":"Kovalerchuk","name":"Kovalerchuk, Boris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brent D.","familyName":"Fegley","name":"Fegley, Brent D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Enhancement with Domain Expert Mental Model to Reduce LLM Hallucination with Causal Prompt Engineering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T14:35:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:18:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-17T23:37:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:16:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When consequential decisions depend on knowledge that exists nowhere in writing, LLMs hallucinate not from retrieval failure but from model absence. RAG and knowledge-graph methods share a structural ceiling. They cannot supply what has never been recorded. This paper reconceptualizes that failure as a model discovery problem. The proposed causal prompt engineering framework discovers and encodes a decision logic of a domain expert as an Expert Mental Model (EMM), providing it as principled input to LLM reasoning supplying what no retrieval method can. The framework is grounded in the theory of monotone Boolean and k-valued functions. A key technical contribution is the formalization of three LLM assisted prerequisite processes previously undeveloped for novel domains: factor formulation, factor monotonization, and monotonicity preserving hierarchical structuring. These enable a four step EMM construction algorithm that reduces elicitation from millions of combinations to a tractable, formally bounded set manageable by humans. The framework also enables, for the first time, formal measurement of LLM hallucination when expert knowledge is entirely tacit. Analysis shows conditional error rates on true positives can approach 100% for plausible LLM-expert mismatches invisible to prior evaluation methods. The approach is validated across grant proposal evaluation, cybersecurity design, and clinical diagnosis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages,4 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10818","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:48:09Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:48:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:23:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10833","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10833","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10833","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dominic","familyName":"Petrak","name":"Petrak, Dominic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thy Thy","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Thy Thy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iryna","familyName":"Gurevych","name":"Gurevych, Iryna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Automated Error Discovery: A Study in Conversational AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T14:53:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:18:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although LLM-based conversational agents demonstrate strong fluency and coherence, they still produce undesirable behaviors (errors) that are challenging to prevent from reaching users during deployment. Recent research leverages large language models (LLMs) to detect errors and guide response-generation models toward improvement. However, current LLMs struggle to identify errors not explicitly specified in their instructions, such as those arising from updates to the response-generation model or shifts in user behavior. In this work, we introduce Automated Error Discovery, a framework for detecting and defining errors in conversational AI, and propose SEEED (Soft Clustering Extended Encoder-Based Error Detection), as an encoder-based approach to its implementation. We enhance the Soft Nearest Neighbor Loss by amplifying distance weighting for negative samples and introduce Label-Based Sample Ranking to select highly contrastive examples for better representation learning. SEEED outperforms adapted baselines -- including GPT-4o and Phi-4 -- across multiple error-annotated dialogue datasets, improving the accuracy for detecting unknown errors by up to 8 points and demonstrating strong generalization to unknown intent detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 main conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10833","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:48:30Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:48:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:48:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10843","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10843","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10843","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Large Language Models for Evidence-Based Clinical Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T15:03:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:19:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated substantial progress in biomedical and clinical applications, motivating rigorous evaluation of their ability to answer nuanced, evidence-based questions. We curate a multi-source benchmark drawing from Cochrane systematic reviews and clinical guidelines, including structured recommendations from the American Heart Association and narrative guidance used by insurers. Using GPT-4o-mini and GPT-5, we observe consistent performance patterns across sources and clinical domains: accuracy is highest on structured guideline recommendations (90%) and lower on narrative guideline and systematic review questions (60--70%). We also find a strong correlation between accuracy and the citation count of the underlying systematic reviews, where each doubling of citations is associated with roughly a 30% increase in the odds of a correct answer. Models show moderate ability to reason about evidence quality when contextual information is supplied. When we incorporate retrieval-augmented prompting, providing the gold-source abstract raises accuracy on previously incorrect items to 0.79; providing top 3 PubMed abstracts (ranked by semantic relevance) improves accuracy to 0.23, while random abstracts reduce accuracy (0.10, within temperature variation). These effects are mirrored in GPT-4o-mini, underscoring that source clarity and targeted retrieval -- not just model size -- drive performance. Overall, our results highlight both the promise and current limitations of LLMs for evidence-based clinical question answering. Retrieval-augmented prompting emerges as a useful strategy to improve factual accuracy and alignment with source evidence, while stratified evaluation by specialty and question type remains essential to understand current knowledge access and to contextualize model performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10843","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:48:45Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:48:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:48:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10844","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10844","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10844","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GAPrune: Gradient-Alignment Pruning for Domain-Aware Embeddings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T15:03:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:19:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Domain-specific embedding models have shown promise for applications that require specialized semantic understanding, such as coding agents and financial retrieval systems, often achieving higher performance gains than general models. However, state-of-the-art embedding models are typically based on LLMs, which contain billions of parameters, making deployment challenging in resource-constrained environments. Model compression through pruning offers a promising solution, but existing pruning methods treat all parameters uniformly, failing to distinguish between general semantic representations and domain-specific patterns, leading to suboptimal pruning decisions. Thus, we propose GAPrune, a pruning framework that addresses this challenge by considering both domain importance and preserving general linguistic foundation. Our method uses Fisher Information to measure importance and general-domain gradient alignment to assess parameter behavior, then combines these signals using our Domain Alignment Importance (DAI) scoring. Lower DAI scores indicate that the parameter is either less important for the domain task or creates conflicts between domain and general objectives. Experiments on two domain benchmarks, FinMTEB and ChemTEB, show that GAPrune maintains performance within 2.5% of dense models in one-shot pruning at 50% sparsity, while outperforming all baselines. With retraining in 100 steps, GAPrune achieves +4.51% improvement on FinMTEB and +1.73% on ChemTEB, demonstrating that our pruning strategy not only preserves but enhances domain-specific capabilities. Our findings demonstrate that principled pruning strategies can achieve model compression and enhanced domain specialization, providing the research community with a new approach for development."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://github.com/yixuantt/GAPrune"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10844","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:48:46Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:48:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:48:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10875","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10875","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10875","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesse","familyName":"Gardner","name":"Gardner, Jesse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladimir A.","familyName":"Baulin","name":"Baulin, Vladimir A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Is the `Agent' Paradigm a Limiting Framework for Next-Generation Intelligent Systems?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T16:11:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:21:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The concept of the 'agent' has profoundly shaped Artificial Intelligence (AI) research, guiding development from foundational theories to contemporary applications like Large Language Model (LLM)-based systems. This paper critically re-evaluates the necessity and optimality of this agent-centric paradigm. We argue that its persistent conceptual ambiguities and inherent anthropocentric biases may represent a limiting framework. We distinguish between agentic systems (AI inspired by agency, often semi-autonomous, e.g., LLM-based agents), agential systems (fully autonomous, self-producing systems, currently only biological), and non-agentic systems (tools without the impression of agency). Our analysis, based on a systematic review of relevant literature, deconstructs the agent paradigm across various AI frameworks, highlighting challenges in defining and measuring properties like autonomy and goal-directedness. We argue that the 'agentic' framing of many AI systems, while heuristically useful, can be misleading and may obscure the underlying computational mechanisms, particularly in Large Language Models (LLMs). As an alternative, we propose a shift in focus towards frameworks grounded in system-level dynamics, world modeling, and material intelligence. We conclude that investigating non-agentic and systemic frameworks, inspired by complex systems, biology, and unconventional computing, is essential for advancing towards robust, scalable, and potentially non-anthropomorphic forms of general intelligence. This requires not only new architectures but also a fundamental reconsideration of our understanding of intelligence itself, moving beyond the agent metaphor."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10875","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:49:30Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:49:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:49:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10884","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10884","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10884","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingxiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qingxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nav-R1: Reasoning and Navigation in Embodied Scenes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T16:31:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:21:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Embodied navigation requires agents to integrate perception, reasoning, and action for robust interaction in complex 3D environments. Existing approaches often suffer from incoherent and unstable reasoning traces that hinder generalization across diverse environments, and difficulty balancing long-horizon semantic reasoning with low-latency control for real-time navigation. To address these challenges, we propose Nav-R1, an embodied foundation model that unifies reasoning in embodied environments. We first construct Nav-CoT-110K, a large-scale dataset of step-by-step Chains-of-Thought (CoT) for embodied tasks, which enables cold-start initialization with structured reasoning. Building on this foundation, we design a GRPO-based reinforcement learning framework with three complementary rewards: format, understanding, and navigation, to improve structural adherence, semantic grounding, and path fidelity. Furthermore, we introduce a Fast-in-Slow reasoning paradigm, decoupling deliberate semantic reasoning from low-latency reactive control for efficient yet coherent navigation. Extensive evaluations on embodied AI benchmarks demonstrate that Nav-R1 consistently outperforms strong baselines, with over 8% average improvement in reasoning and navigation performance. Real-world deployment on a mobile robot further validates its robustness under limited onboard resources. Code: https://github.com/AIGeeksGroup/Nav-R1. Website: https://aigeeksgroup.github.io/Nav-R1."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10884","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:49:42Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:49:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:49:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10886","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10886","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10886","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jialong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baosong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Baosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CultureSynth: A Hierarchical Taxonomy-Guided and Retrieval-Augmented Framework for Cultural Question-Answer Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T16:33:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:21:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cultural competence, defined as the ability to understand and adapt to multicultural contexts, is increasingly vital for large language models (LLMs) in global environments. While several cultural benchmarks exist to assess LLMs' cultural competence, current evaluations suffer from fragmented taxonomies, domain specificity, and heavy reliance on manual data annotation. To address these limitations, we introduce CultureSynth, a novel framework comprising (1) a comprehensive hierarchical multilingual cultural taxonomy covering 12 primary and 130 secondary topics, and (2) a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)-based methodology leveraging factual knowledge to synthesize culturally relevant question-answer pairs. The CultureSynth-7 synthetic benchmark contains 19,360 entries and 4,149 manually verified entries across 7 languages. Evaluation of 14 prevalent LLMs of different sizes reveals clear performance stratification led by ChatGPT-4o-Latest and Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct. The results demonstrate that a 3B-parameter threshold is necessary for achieving basic cultural competence, models display varying architectural biases in knowledge processing, and significant geographic disparities exist across models. We believe that CultureSynth offers a scalable framework for developing culturally aware AI systems while reducing reliance on manual annotation\\footnote{Benchmark is available at https://github.com/Eyr3/CultureSynth.}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a Findings paper at EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10886","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:49:45Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:49:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:49:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10931","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10931","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10931","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seongho","familyName":"Joo","name":"Joo, Seongho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyukhun","familyName":"Koh","name":"Koh, Hyukhun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyomin","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Kyomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Harmful Prompt Laundering: Jailbreaking LLMs with Abductive Styles and Symbolic Encoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T18:07:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:24:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across diverse tasks, but their potential misuse for harmful purposes remains a significant concern. To strengthen defenses against such vulnerabilities, it is essential to investigate universal jailbreak attacks that exploit intrinsic weaknesses in the architecture and learning paradigms of LLMs. In response, we propose \\textbf{H}armful \\textbf{P}rompt \\textbf{La}undering (HaPLa), a novel and broadly applicable jailbreaking technique that requires only black-box access to target models. HaPLa incorporates two primary strategies: 1) \\textit{abductive framing}, which instructs LLMs to infer plausible intermediate steps toward harmful activities, rather than directly responding to explicit harmful queries; and 2) \\textit{symbolic encoding}, a lightweight and flexible approach designed to obfuscate harmful content, given that current LLMs remain sensitive primarily to explicit harmful keywords. Experimental results show that HaPLa achieves over 95% attack success rate on GPT-series models and 70% across all targets. Further analysis with diverse symbolic encoding rules also reveals a fundamental challenge: it remains difficult to safely tune LLMs without significantly diminishing their helpfulness in responding to benign queries."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10931","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:50:49Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:50:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:50:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10970","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10970","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10970","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua Au","familyName":"Yeung","name":"Yeung, Joshua Au","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacopo","familyName":"Dalmasso","name":"Dalmasso, Jacopo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Foschini","name":"Foschini, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard JB","familyName":"Dobson","name":"Dobson, Richard JB","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeljko","familyName":"Kraljevic","name":"Kraljevic, Zeljko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Psychogenic Machine: Simulating AI Psychosis, Delusion Reinforcement and Harm Enablement in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T20:10:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:26:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T01:44:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:15:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Background: Emerging reports of \"AI psychosis\" are on the rise, where user-LLM interactions may exacerbate or induce psychosis or adverse psychological symptoms. Whilst the sycophantic and agreeable nature of LLMs can be beneficial, it becomes a vector for harm by reinforcing delusional beliefs in vulnerable users.\n Methods: Psychosis-bench is a novel benchmark designed to systematically evaluate the psychogenicity of LLMs comprises 16 structured, 12-turn conversational scenarios simulating the progression of delusional themes(Erotic Delusions, Grandiose/Messianic Delusions, Referential Delusions) and potential harms. We evaluated eight prominent LLMs for Delusion Confirmation (DCS), Harm Enablement (HES), and Safety Intervention(SIS) across explicit and implicit conversational contexts.\n Findings: Across 1,536 simulated conversation turns, all LLMs demonstrated psychogenic potential, showing a strong tendency to perpetuate rather than challenge delusions (mean DCS of 0.91 $\\pm$0.88). Models frequently enabled harmful user requests (mean HES of 0.69 $\\pm$0.84) and offered safety interventions in only roughly a third of applicable turns (mean SIS of 0.37 $\\pm$0.48). 51 / 128 (39.8%) of scenarios had no safety interventions offered. Performance was significantly worse in implicit scenarios, models were more likely to confirm delusions and enable harm while offering fewer interventions (p \u0026lt; .001). A strong correlation was found between DCS and HES (rs = .77). Model performance varied widely, indicating that safety is not an emergent property of scale alone.\n Conclusion: This study establishes LLM psychogenicity as a quantifiable risk and underscores the urgent need for re-thinking how we train LLMs. We frame this issue not merely as a technical challenge but as a public health imperative requiring collaboration between developers, policymakers, and healthcare professionals."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10970","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:51:43Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:51:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:43:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10975","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10975","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10975","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jielong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jielong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenxing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxing","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jianxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ReFineG: Synergizing Small Supervised Models and LLMs for Low-Resource Grounded Multimodal NER"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T20:32:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:27:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-12T08:01:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:31:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Grounded Multimodal Named Entity Recognition (GMNER) extends traditional NER by jointly detecting textual mentions and grounding them to visual regions. While existing supervised methods achieve strong performance, they rely on costly multimodal annotations and often underperform in low-resource domains. Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) show strong generalization but suffer from Domain Knowledge Conflict, producing redundant or incorrect mentions for domain-specific entities. To address these challenges, we propose ReFineG, a three-stage collaborative framework that integrates small supervised models with frozen MLLMs for low-resource GMNER. In the Training Stage, a domain-aware NER data synthesis strategy transfers LLM knowledge to small models with supervised training while avoiding domain knowledge conflicts. In the Refinement Stage, an uncertainty-based mechanism retains confident predictions from supervised models and delegates uncertain ones to the MLLM. In the Grounding Stage, a multimodal context selection algorithm enhances visual grounding through analogical reasoning. In the CCKS2025 GMNER Shared Task, ReFineG ranked second with an F1 score of 0.6461 on the online leaderboard, demonstrating its effectiveness with limited annotations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CCKS 2025 Shared Task Paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10975","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:51:50Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:51:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-13T03:08:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11026","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11026","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11026","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manasi","familyName":"Ganti","name":"Ganti, Manasi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixian","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Zixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helena","familyName":"Vasconcelos","name":"Vasconcelos, Helena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qijia","familyName":"He","name":"He, Qijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ranjay","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Ranjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Human Preference Evaluation of LLM Rationales"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T01:33:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:30:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) often generate natural language rationales -- free-form explanations that help improve performance on complex reasoning tasks and enhance interpretability for human users. However, evaluating these rationales remains challenging. While recent work has relied on binary preference judgments from humans or LLM judges, such evaluations are often opaque and coarse-grained, offering limited insight into what makes one rationale better than another. In this work, we rethink preference evaluation for LLM-generated rationales by asking: (1) What attributes define good rationales? (2) Can human preferences be explained by these attributes? (3) Can attribute-based evaluation overcome the limitations of binary comparisons? We identify a set of key rationale attributes from prior literature and assess them using automatic metrics, LLM judgments, and human annotations. We then analyze two standard human preference datasets MT Bench and Chatbot Arena using SHAP to identify which attributes best explain human preference outcomes. Finally, we re-evaluate model-generated rationales using attribute-specific ELO scores, revealing more nuanced model comparisons and insights. Our findings suggest that fine-grained attribute evaluations can better characterize rationale quality and guide future research toward more interpretable and reliable evaluation practices."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in the XLLM-Reason-Plan Workshop on the Application of LLM Explainability to Reasoning and Planning at COLM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11026","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:53:02Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:53:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:53:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11035","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11035","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11035","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Licheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Licheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Zuo","name":"Zuo, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Free-MAD: Consensus-Free Multi-Agent Debate"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T01:55:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:31:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent debate (MAD) is an emerging approach to improving the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs). Existing MAD methods rely on multiple rounds of interaction among agents to reach consensus, and the final output is selected by majority voting in the last round. However, this consensus-based design faces several limitations. First, multiple rounds of communication increases token overhead and limits scalability. Second, due to the inherent conformity of LLMs, agents that initially produce correct responses may be influenced by incorrect ones during the debate process, causing error propagation. Third, majority voting introduces randomness and unfairness in the decision-making phase, and can degrade the reasoning performance.\n To address these issues, we propose \\textsc{Free-MAD}, a novel MAD framework that eliminates the need for consensus among agents. \\textsc{Free-MAD} introduces a novel score-based decision mechanism that evaluates the entire debate trajectory rather than relying on the last round only. This mechanism tracks how each agent's reasoning evolves, enabling more accurate and fair outcomes. In addition, \\textsc{Free-MAD} reconstructs the debate phase by introducing anti-conformity, a mechanism that enables agents to mitigate excessive influence from the majority. Experiments on eight benchmark datasets demonstrate that \\textsc{Free-MAD} significantly improves reasoning performance while requiring only a single-round debate and thus reducing token costs. We also show that compared to existing MAD approaches, \\textsc{Free-MAD} exhibits improved robustness in real-world attack scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11035","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:53:15Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:53:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:53:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11052","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11052","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11052","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dai","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Dai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Chuai","name":"Chuai, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingruo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingruo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriele","familyName":"Lenzini","name":"Lenzini, Gabriele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hewu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hewu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fact-Checking Comments Precede Community Notes to Misleading Posts on X"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T02:40:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:32:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:13:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:21:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Community-based fact-checking systems, such as X's Community Notes program, offer a potentially scalable approach against online misinformation diffusion. However, their efficacy is substantially undermined by the delay of fact-check delivery. To address this challenge, we investigated the promise of organic user comments as rapid corrective signals by analyzing a large-scale dataset of over 2.2 million comments directed to 1,841 community fact-checked misleading posts. We developed a high-performance language model pipeline to identify ``fact-checking (FC) comments'', i.e., comments that correct source posts with reasoning or evidence (91% accuracy). Using this pipeline, we find that 99.4% of misleading posts receive their first FC comments before the official community note is created. Notably, the median time to the initial FC comment is only 0.1 hours, while the creation of community notes has a median latency of 9.7 hours since the publication of misleading posts. Additionally, compared to random posts, those misleading posts with displayed notes have significantly more FC comments. This suggests that FC comments are a unique pattern for misleading posts. We further identified characteristics of FC comments' intensity and speed: (i) FC comments' volume is significantly associated with content richness and misinformation type, favoring multimedia content and missing-context scenarios; (ii) the speed of FC comments remains robust across diverse topics and emotional intensities. These results suggest that platforms could use FC comments as early-warning signals, and potential resource for complementing community notes or automated synthesis."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11052","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:53:39Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:53:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T02:59:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11062","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11062","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11062","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yuheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjia","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Wenjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:05:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:13:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T11:06:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:38:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-01T08:41:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-02T00:37:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid progress of large language models (LLMs) has opened new opportunities for education. While learners can interact with academic papers through LLM-powered dialogue, limitations still exist: the lack of structured organization and the heavy reliance on text can impede systematic understanding and engagement with complex concepts. To address these challenges, we propose Auto-Slides, an LLM-driven system that converts research papers into pedagogically structured, multimodal slides (e.g., diagrams and tables). Drawing on cognitive science, it creates a presentation-oriented narrative and allows iterative refinement via an interactive editor to better match learners' knowledge level and goals. Auto-Slides further incorporates verification and knowledge retrieval mechanisms to ensure accuracy and contextual completeness. Through extensive user studies, Auto-Slides demonstrates strong learner acceptance, improved structural support for understanding, and expert-validated gains in narrative quality compared with conventional LLM-based reading. Our contributions lie in designing a multi-agent framework for transforming academic papers into pedagogically optimized slides and introducing interactive customization for personalized learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Homepage: https://auto-slides.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11062","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:53:52Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:53:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-02T02:27:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11068","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11068","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11068","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zan-Kai","familyName":"Chong","name":"Chong, Zan-Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroyuki","familyName":"Ohsaki","name":"Ohsaki, Hiroyuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tractable Asymmetric Verification for Large Language Models via Deterministic Replicability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:30:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:32:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The landscape of Large Language Models (LLMs) shifts rapidly towards dynamic, multi-agent systems. This introduces a fundamental challenge in establishing computational trust, specifically how one agent can verify that another's output was genuinely produced by a claimed LLM, and not falsified or generated by a cheaper or inferior model. To address this challenge, this paper proposes a verification framework that achieves tractable asymmetric effort, where the cost to verify a computation is substantially lower than the cost to perform it. Our approach is built upon the principle of deterministic replicability, a property inherent to autoregressive models that strictly necessitates a computationally homogeneous environment where all agents operate on identical hardware and software stacks. Within this defined context, our framework enables multiple validators to probabilistically audit small, random segments of an LLM's output and it distributes the verification workload effectively. The simulations demonstrated that targeted verification can be over 12 times faster than full regeneration, with tunable parameters to adjust the detection probability. By establishing a tractable mechanism for auditable LLM systems, our work offers a foundational layer for responsible AI and serves as a cornerstone for future research into the more complex, heterogeneous multi-agent systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11068","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:00Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:54:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11071","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11071","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11071","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghan","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Jinghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dehui","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Dehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The System Description of CPS Team for Track on Driving with Language of CVPR 2024 Autonomous Grand Challenge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:37:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:32:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This report outlines our approach using vision language model systems for the Driving with Language track of the CVPR 2024 Autonomous Grand Challenge. We have exclusively utilized the DriveLM-nuScenes dataset for training our models. Our systems are built on the LLaVA models, which we enhanced through fine-tuning with the LoRA and DoRA methods. Additionally, we have integrated depth information from open-source depth estimation models to enrich the training and inference processes. For inference, particularly with multiple-choice and yes/no questions, we adopted a Chain-of-Thought reasoning approach to improve the accuracy of the results. This comprehensive methodology enabled us to achieve a top score of 0.7799 on the validation set leaderboard, ranking 1st on the leaderboard."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11071","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:05Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:54:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11076","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11076","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11076","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zibo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shipeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinjing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xinjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chendong","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Chendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingxu","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Bingxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiheng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"She","name":"She, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"You","name":"You, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghuan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Guanghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanchun","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Wanchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guihai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SmartSwap: Swap-Based Memory Optimization for LLM Training under Varying Operator Sequences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:55:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:33:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-15T13:51:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-16T00:48:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The increasing size of large language models (LLMs) has led to a surge in memory requirements during training, often exceeding the capacity of high-bandwidth memory (HBM). Swap-based memory optimization incurs neither accuracy loss nor additional end-to-end overhead when effectively overlapped, thus being an attractive solution. However, existing swap methods assume consistent operator sequences, which is impractical in Eager Mode, where operator sequences can vary during change.\n We propose Chameleon, which redesigns the end-to-end process of swap-based memory optimization and is the first work to consider varying operator sequences in Eager Mode. Chameleon (i) introduces a lightweight online profiler to enable continuous profiling for monitoring operator sequences, (ii) generates effective swap policies with limited operator information, and (iii) optimizes the policy execution module for accurate policy application and better performance. Experimental results demonstrate that Chameleon reduces profiling overhead by 84.25%, enables training models up to 4x larger than hardware memory while adapting to changes in operator sequences, improves performance by up to 38.94% compared to recomputation or high-degree parallelism."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to DAC 2026. Previously titled \"Chameleon: Taming Dynamic Operator Sequences for Memory-Intensive LLM Training.\""}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11076","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:12Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-16T02:33:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11078","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11078","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11078","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunghwei","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Yunghwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Weizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Patient-Zero: Scaling Synthetic Patient Agents to Real-World Distributions without Real Patient Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:56:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:33:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-06T13:16:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:42:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Synthetic data generation with Large Language Models (LLMs) has emerged as a promising solution in the medical domain to mitigate data scarcity and privacy constraints. However, existing approaches remain constrained by their derivative nature, relying on real-world records, which pose privacy risks and distribution biases. Furthermore, current patient agents face the Stability-Plasticity Dilemma, struggling to maintain clinical consistency during dynamic inquiries. To address these challenges, we introduce Patient-Zero, a novel framework for ab initio patient simulation that requires no real medical records. Our Medically-Aligned Hierarchical Synthesis framework generates comprehensive and diverse patient records from abstract clinical guidelines via stratified attribute permutation. To support rigorous clinical interaction, we design a Dual-Track Cognitive Memory System to enable agents dynamically update memory while preserving logical consistency and persona adherence. Extensive evaluations show that Patient-Zero establishes a new state-of-the-art in both data quality and interaction fidelity. In human expert evaluations, senior licensed physicians judge our synthetic data to be statistically indistinguishable from real human-authored data and higher in clinical quality. Furthermore, downstream medical reasoning model trained on our synthetic dataset shows substantial performance gains (MedQA +24.0%; MMLU +14.5%), demonstrating the practical utility of our framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11078","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:15Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-07T02:59:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11079","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11079","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11079","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwei","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jinwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizhen","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Qizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinghui","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yinghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lifan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyou","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Weiyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lewei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Lewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Jingsong","name":"Jingsong, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Difficulty-Aware Agentic Orchestration for Query-Specific Multi-Agent Workflows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:57:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T02:48:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T13:32:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:52:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-11T12:33:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:35:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-08T06:37:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-09T02:14:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-13T16:13:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-02-16T01:50:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM)-based agentic systems have shown strong capabilities across various tasks. However, existing multi-agent frameworks often rely on static or task-level workflows, which either over-process simple queries or underperform on complex ones, while also neglecting the efficiency-performance trade-offs across heterogeneous LLMs. To address these limitations, we propose Difficulty-Aware Agentic Orchestration (DAAO), which can dynamically generate query-specific multi-agent workflows guided by predicted query difficulty. DAAO comprises three interdependent modules: a variational autoencoder (VAE) for difficulty estimation, a modular operator allocator, and a cost- and performance-aware LLM router. A self-adjusting policy updates difficulty estimates based on workflow success, enabling simpler workflows for easy queries and more complex strategies for harder ones. Experiments on six benchmarks demonstrate that DAAO surpasses prior multi-agent systems in both accuracy and inference efficiency, validating its effectiveness for adaptive, difficulty-aware reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to WWW2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11079","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-16T02:59:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11101","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11101","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11101","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haokun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haokun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yazhou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yazhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jizhi","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Jizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuchi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qiuchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeing is Not Understanding: A Benchmark on Perception-Cognition Disparities in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T05:40:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:34:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T08:45:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:20:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T02:12:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:22:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs), they have demonstrated exceptional capabilities across a variety of vision-language tasks. However, current evaluation benchmarks predominantly focus on objective visual question answering or captioning, inadequately assessing the models' ability to understand complex and subjective human emotions. To bridge this gap, we introduce EmoBench-Reddit, a novel, hierarchical benchmark for multimodal emotion understanding. The dataset comprises 350 meticulously curated samples from the social media platform Reddit, each containing an image, associated user-provided text, and an emotion category (sad, humor, sarcasm, happy) confirmed by user flairs. We designed a hierarchical task framework that progresses from basic perception to advanced cognition, with each data point featuring six multiple-choice questions and one open-ended question of increasing difficulty. Perception tasks evaluate the model's ability to identify basic visual elements (e.g., colors, objects), while cognition tasks require scene reasoning, intent understanding, and deep empathy integrating textual context. We ensured annotation quality through a combination of AI assistance (Claude 4) and manual verification.We conducted a comprehensive evaluation of nine leading MLLMs, including GPT-5, Gemini-2.5-pro, and GPT-4o, on EmoBench-Reddit."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"I need to modify the content of the article"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11101","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:46Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T02:35:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11118","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11118","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11118","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Priyanshu","familyName":"Priya","name":"Priya, Priyanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurav","familyName":"Dudhate","name":"Dudhate, Saurav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Desai Vishesh","familyName":"Yasheshbhai","name":"Yasheshbhai, Desai Vishesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asif","familyName":"Ekbal","name":"Ekbal, Asif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"We Argue to Agree: Towards Personality-Driven Argumentation-Based Negotiation Dialogue Systems for Tourism"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T06:16:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:35:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integrating argumentation mechanisms into negotiation dialogue systems improves conflict resolution through exchanges of arguments and critiques. Moreover, incorporating personality attributes enhances adaptability by aligning interactions with individuals' preferences and styles. To advance these capabilities in negotiation dialogue systems, we propose a novel Personality-driven Argumentation-based Negotiation Dialogue Generation (PAN-DG) task. To support this task, we introduce PACT, a dataset of Personality-driven Argumentation-based negotiation Conversations for Tourism sector. This dataset, generated using Large Language Models (LLMs), features three distinct personality profiles, viz. Argumentation Profile, Preference Profile, and Buying Style Profile to simulate a variety of negotiation scenarios involving diverse personalities. Thorough automatic and manual evaluations indicate that the dataset comprises high-quality dialogues. Further, we conduct comparative experiments between pre-trained and fine-tuned LLMs for the PAN-DG task. Multi-dimensional evaluation demonstrates that the fine-tuned LLMs effectively generate personality-driven rational responses during negotiations. This underscores the effectiveness of PACT in enhancing personalization and reasoning capabilities in negotiation dialogue systems, thereby establishing a foundation for future research in this domain."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Paper is accepted at EMNLP (Findings) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11118","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:55:10Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:55:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:55:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11120","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11120","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11120","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingzhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qingzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaocheng","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Shaocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Z. Morley","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Z. Morley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miroslav","familyName":"Pajic","name":"Pajic, Miroslav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael K.","familyName":"Reiter","name":"Reiter, Michael K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SoK: How Sensor Attacks Disrupt Autonomous Vehicles: An End-to-end Analysis, Challenges, and Missed Threats"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T06:19:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:35:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T04:55:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:23:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T01:59:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:16:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-17T17:57:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-18T03:05:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-07T06:29:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-09T01:26:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous vehicles, including self-driving cars, ground robots, and drones, rely on multi-modal sensor pipelines for safe operation, yet remain vulnerable to adversarial sensor attacks. A critical gap is the lack of a systematic end-to-end view of how sensor-induced errors traverse interconnected modules to affect the physical world. To address this gap, we provide a comprehensive survey across platforms, sensing modalities, attack methods, and countermeasures. At its core is Systematic Error Propagation Graph (SEPG), a graph-based framework that maps how attacks inject errors, the conditions for their propagation through modules from perception and localization to planning and control, and when they reach physical impact. From this analysis, our study distills 8 key findings that highlight the feasibility constraints of sensor attacks and uncovers 12 previously overlooked attack vectors exploiting inter-module interactions, several of which we validate via proof-of-concept experiments. We further conduct a pilot user study with autonomous vehicle security researchers, showing that SEPG is viewed as a faithful and useful framework for qualitative system-level attack analysis. We also evaluate large language models (LLMs) for generating the analysis, demonstrating the potential of AI-powered automation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11120","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:55:12Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:55:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T05:53:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11124","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11124","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11124","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tutti","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Tutti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Letian","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Letian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yixiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"STASE: A spatialized text-to-audio synthesis engine for music generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T06:29:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T04:51:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While many text-to-audio systems produce monophonic or fixed-stereo outputs, generating audio with user-defined spatial properties remains a challenge. Existing deep learning-based spatialization methods often rely on latent-space manipulations, which can limit direct control over psychoacoustic parameters critical to spatial perception. To address this, we introduce STASE, a system that leverages a Large Language Model (LLM) as an agent to interpret spatial cues from text. A key feature of STASE is the decoupling of semantic interpretation from a separate, physics-based spatial rendering engine, which facilitates interpretable and user-controllable spatial reasoning. The LLM processes prompts through two main pathways: (i) Description Prompts, for direct mapping of explicit spatial information (e.g., \"place the lead guitar at 45° azimuth, 10 m distance\"), and (ii) Abstract Prompts, where a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) module retrieves relevant spatial templates to inform the rendering. This paper details the STASE workflow, discusses implementation considerations, and highlights current challenges in evaluating generative spatial audio."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to LLM4Music @ ISMIR 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11124","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:55:18Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:55:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:12:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11127","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11127","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11127","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hongxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hylke","familyName":"Westerdijk","name":"Westerdijk, Hylke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khondoker Ittehadul","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Khondoker Ittehadul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Joint Effects of Argumentation Theory, Audio Modality and Data Enrichment on LLM-Based Fallacy Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T06:35:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:35:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study investigates how context and emotional tone metadata influence large language model (LLM) reasoning and performance in fallacy classification tasks, particularly within political debate settings. Using data from U.S. presidential debates, we classify six fallacy types through various prompting strategies applied to the Qwen-3 (8B) model. We introduce two theoretically grounded Chain-of-Thought frameworks: Pragma-Dialectics and the Periodic Table of Arguments, and evaluate their effectiveness against a baseline prompt under three input settings: text-only, text with context, and text with both context and audio-based emotional tone metadata. Results suggest that while theoretical prompting can improve interpretability and, in some cases, accuracy, the addition of context and especially emotional tone metadata often leads to lowered performance. Emotional tone metadata biases the model toward labeling statements as \\textit{Appeal to Emotion}, worsening logical reasoning. Overall, basic prompts often outperformed enhanced ones, suggesting that attention dilution from added inputs may worsen rather than improve fallacy classification in LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11127","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:55:22Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:55:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:55:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11141","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11141","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11141","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyao","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Shiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xijia","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingkang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yingkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxiao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Junxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhexin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biplab","familyName":"Sikdar","name":"Sikdar, Biplab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongning","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minlie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Minlie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Smiley Turns Hostile: Interpreting How Emojis Trigger LLMs' Toxicity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T07:21:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:36:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Emojis are globally used non-verbal cues in digital communication, and extensive research has examined how large language models (LLMs) understand and utilize emojis across contexts. While usually associated with friendliness or playfulness, it is observed that emojis may trigger toxic content generation in LLMs. Motivated by such a observation, we aim to investigate: (1) whether emojis can clearly enhance the toxicity generation in LLMs and (2) how to interpret this phenomenon. We begin with a comprehensive exploration of emoji-triggered LLM toxicity generation by automating the construction of prompts with emojis to subtly express toxic intent. Experiments across 5 mainstream languages on 7 famous LLMs along with jailbreak tasks demonstrate that prompts with emojis could easily induce toxicity generation. To understand this phenomenon, we conduct model-level interpretations spanning semantic cognition, sequence generation and tokenization, suggesting that emojis can act as a heterogeneous semantic channel to bypass the safety mechanisms. To pursue deeper insights, we further probe the pre-training corpus and uncover potential correlation between the emoji-related data polution with the toxicity generation behaviors. Supplementary materials provide our implementation code and data. (Warning: This paper contains potentially sensitive contents)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11141","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:55:42Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:55:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:55:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lihai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Lihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Boyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gongyi","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Gongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kerun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Kerun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiyu","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Feiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Siheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Text2Mem: A Unified Memory Operation Language for Memory Operating System"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T07:30:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T17:38:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T17:53:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T01:03:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model agents increasingly depend on memory to sustain long horizon interaction, but existing frameworks remain limited. Most expose only a few basic primitives such as encode, retrieve, and delete, while higher order operations like merge, promote, demote, split, lock, and expire are missing or inconsistently supported. Moreover, there is no formal and executable specification for memory commands, leaving scope and lifecycle rules implicit and causing unpredictable behavior across systems. We introduce Text2Mem, a unified memory operation language that provides a standardized pathway from natural language to reliable execution. Text2Mem defines a compact yet expressive operation set aligned with encoding, storage, and retrieval. Each instruction is represented as a JSON based schema instance with required fields and semantic invariants, which a parser transforms into typed operation objects with normalized parameters. A validator ensures correctness before execution, while adapters map typed objects either to a SQL prototype backend or to real memory frameworks. Model based services such as embeddings or summarization are integrated when required. All results are returned through a unified execution contract. This design ensures safety, determinism, and portability across heterogeneous backends. We also outline Text2Mem Bench, a planned benchmark that separates schema generation from backend execution to enable systematic evaluation. Together, these components establish the first standardized foundation for memory control in agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 3 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:55:48Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:55:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-24T02:14:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11155","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11155","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11155","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santhosh G","familyName":"S","name":"S, Santhosh G","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurav","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Saurav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Balaraman","familyName":"Ravindran","name":"Ravindran, Balaraman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AQUA: Attention via QUery mAgnitudes for Memory and Compute Efficient Inference in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T08:20:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:38:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The quadratic complexity of the attention mechanism remains a fundamental barrier to scaling Large Language Models (LLMs) to longer contexts, creating a critical bottleneck in both computation and memory. To address this, we introduce AQUA (Attention via QUery mAgnitudes) a novel and versatile approximation strategy that significantly reduces the cost of attention with a graceful performance trade-off. Our method operates in two phases: an efficient offline step where we compute a universal, language agnostic projection matrix via SVD on a calibration dataset, and an online inference step where we project query and key vectors and dynamically select a sparse subset of dimensions based on the query's magnitude. We provide a formal theoretical analysis of AQUA, establishing the break-even point at which it becomes more computationally efficient than standard attention. Our empirical evaluations on state-of-the-art models like Llama-3.1-8B demonstrate that a 25% reduction in the attention dot-product computation can be achieved with a statistically insignificant impact on performance across a wide range of benchmarks. We further showcase the versatility of AQUA by demonstrating its ability to synergistically accelerate existing token eviction methods like H2O and to directly reduce KV-cache memory size. By offering a controllable knob to balance efficiency and accuracy, AQUA provides a practical and powerful tool for making large-scale LLM inference more accessible and sustainable."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11155","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:56:02Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:56:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:56:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11165","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11165","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11165","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Waikit","familyName":"Xiu","name":"Xiu, Waikit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingchen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiying","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Traffic-MLLM: Curiosity-Regularized Supervised Learning for Traffic Scenario Case-Based Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T08:53:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:38:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T14:12:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:45:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For safe and robust autonomous driving, decision-making systems must effectively leverage past experiences to handle the inherent long-tail of traffic scenarios. Case-Based Reasoning (CBR) provides a natural paradigm for this by adapting solutions from prior cases. However, in complex and dynamic traffic environments, traditional CBR methods struggle to effectively abstract and adapt knowledge under uncertainty. Meanwhile, although multimodal large language models (MLLMs) exhibit strong perceptual and linguistic capabilities, their reasoning behavior often relies on empirical pattern fitting, limiting robustness under distribution shift and long-tail scenarios. We propose Traffic-MLLM, a retrieval-free neural case modeling framework for multimodal traffic reasoning. Instead of performing explicit case retrieval at inference time, Traffic-MLLM learns a structured and generalizable case space directly during training. To support this learning process, we construct a multi-source case base by integrating dynamic traffic videos and large-scale static visual question-answering data, serving as a unified training substrate for learning structured case representations. To further improve representation quality near knowledge boundaries, we introduce a curiosity-driven refinement mechanism based on Random Network Distillation (RND), encouraging the model to internalize cross-case structural regularities rather than surface correlations. Experiments on the SUTD-TrafficQA and DriveQA benchmarks demonstrate consistent improvements in dynamic reasoning, regulatory understanding, and cross-domain transfer. Traffic-MLLM achieves 50.8% accuracy on SUTD-TrafficQA, 74.8% on the CARLA-based DriveQA split, and 83.1% on the real-world Mapillary split, indicating that representation-level case-space refinement provides an effective alternative to explicit retrieval for scalable multimodal case adaptation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11165","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:56:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:56:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:49:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11167","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11167","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11167","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pouria","familyName":"Mahdavinia","name":"Mahdavinia, Pouria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamed","familyName":"Mahdavi","name":"Mahdavi, Hamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niloofar","familyName":"Mireshghallah","name":"Mireshghallah, Niloofar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehrdad","familyName":"Mahdavi","name":"Mahdavi, Mehrdad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Harnessing Optimization Dynamics for Curvature-Informed Model Merging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T08:59:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:39:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Model merging is an effective post-training strategy for composing capabilities in large language models without joint retraining. We study this in the supervised fine-tuning (SFT) stage, where multiple capability-based SFT checkpoints -- spanning math, code, precise instruction following, general instruction following, and knowledge recall -- must be consolidated into a single model. We introduce Optimization Trajectory Aware (OTA) Merging, a curvature-aware aggregation that leverages optimizer second-moment statistics as a diagonal curvature proxy to reweight parameter edits and mitigate interference. Complementing OTA, we propose Fast Fisher Grafting (FFG), a curvature-driven task-localization step that sparsifies conflicting or low-importance edits. FFG induces extremely low-rank masks concentrated in early attention query/key projections and token embeddings, exploiting shared curvature across capabilities. We further develop a memory-light compression of the second moments that preserves OTA's effect. Across diverse capability-based SFT checkpoints, OTA+FFG improves merged-model quality over strong weight-space baselines, reduces negative transfer, and remains robust across sparsity levels. Analyses reveal substantial curvature overlap between checkpoints, offering a novel lens on why simple linear merging can be effective in practice. Ablations confirm that FFG is critical for reducing task interference and that the compressed second moments retain the gains of the full formulation. To facilitate reproducibility, we open-source all code, training and evaluation scripts, visualization artifacts, and capability-specific SFT checkpoints at https://github.com/pmahdavi/ota-merge."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11167","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:56:20Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:56:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:56:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11177","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11177","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11177","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yawei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Benini","name":"Benini, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal Brain Restoration for Joint Quantization and Sparsification of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T09:17:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:39:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T03:17:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:18:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Large Language Model (LLM) compression, such as quantization and pruning, have achieved notable success. However, as these techniques gradually approach their respective limits, relying on a single method for further compression has become increasingly challenging. In this work, we explore an alternative solution by combining quantization and sparsity. This joint approach, though promising, introduces new difficulties due to the inherently conflicting requirements on weight distributions: quantization favors compact ranges, while pruning benefits from high variance. To attack this problem, we propose Optimal Brain Restoration (OBR), a general and training-free framework that aligns pruning and quantization by error compensation between both. OBR minimizes performance degradation on downstream tasks by building on a second-order Hessian objective, which is then reformulated into a tractable problem through surrogate approximation and ultimately reaches a closed-form solution via group error compensation. Experiments show that OBR enables aggressive W4A4KV4 quantization with 50% sparsity on existing LLMs, and delivers up to 4.72x speedup and 6.4x memory reduction compared to the FP16-dense baseline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11177","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:56:33Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:56:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:12:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11183","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11183","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11183","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanouil","familyName":"Karystinaios","name":"Karystinaios, Emmanouil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WeaveMuse: An Open Agentic System for Multimodal Music Understanding and Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T09:29:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:39:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic AI has been standardized in industry as a practical paradigm for coordinating specialized models and tools to solve complex multimodal tasks. In this work, we present WeaveMuse, a multi-agent system for music understanding, symbolic composition, and audio synthesis. Each specialist agent interprets user requests, derives machine-actionable requirements (modalities, formats, constraints), and validates its own outputs, while a manager agent selects and sequences tools, mediates user interaction, and maintains state across turns. The system is extendable and deployable either locally, using quantization and inference strategies to fit diverse hardware budgets, or via the HFApi to preserve free community access to open models. Beyond out-of-the-box use, the system emphasizes controllability and adaptation through constraint schemas, structured decoding, policy-based inference, and parameter-efficient adapters or distilled variants that tailor models to MIR tasks. A central design goal is to facilitate intermodal interaction across text, symbolic notation and visualization, and audio, enabling analysis-synthesis-render loops and addressing cross-format constraints. The framework aims to democratize, implement, and make accessible MIR tools by supporting interchangeable open-source models of various sizes, flexible memory management, and reproducible deployment paths."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at Large Language Models for Music \u0026amp; Audio Workshop (LLM4MA) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11183","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:56:41Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:56:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:56:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11197","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11197","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11197","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yunheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuetong","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yuetong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taowen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Taowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yixiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yawen","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiran","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Peiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiding","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Yiding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renjing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Renjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DreamNav: A Trajectory-Based Imaginative Framework for Zero-Shot Vision-and-Language Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T09:54:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:40:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-and-Language Navigation in Continuous Environments (VLN-CE), which links language instructions to perception and control in the real world, is a core capability of embodied robots. Recently, large-scale pretrained foundation models have been leveraged as shared priors for perception, reasoning, and action, enabling zero-shot VLN without task-specific training. However, existing zero-shot VLN methods depend on costly perception and passive scene understanding, collapsing control to point-level choices. As a result, they are expensive to deploy, misaligned in action semantics, and short-sighted in planning. To address these issues, we present DreamNav that focuses on the following three aspects: (1) for reducing sensory cost, our EgoView Corrector aligns viewpoints and stabilizes egocentric perception; (2) instead of point-level actions, our Trajectory Predictor favors global trajectory-level planning to better align with instruction semantics; and (3) to enable anticipatory and long-horizon planning, we propose an Imagination Predictor to endow the agent with proactive thinking capability. On VLN-CE and real-world tests, DreamNav sets a new zero-shot state-of-the-art (SOTA), outperforming the strongest egocentric baseline with extra information by up to 7.49\\% and 18.15\\% in terms of SR and SPL metrics. To our knowledge, this is the first zero-shot VLN method to unify trajectory-level planning and active imagination while using only egocentric inputs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11197","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:57:01Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:57:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:57:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11250","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11250","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11250","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yitong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ximo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ximo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyi","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Liyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Environmental Injection Attacks against GUI Agents in Realistic Dynamic Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T12:47:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:43:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-31T08:50:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:34:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-24T12:04:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:35:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graphical User Interface (GUI) agents are increasingly deployed to interact with online web services, yet their exposure to open-world content renders them vulnerable to Environmental Injection Attacks (EIAs). In these attacks, an attacker can inject crafted triggers into website to manipulate the behavior of GUI agents used by other users. In this paper, we find that most existing EIA studies fall short of realism. In particular, they fail to capture the dynamic nature of real-world web content, often assuming that a trigger's on-screen position and surrounding visual context remain largely consistent between training and testing. To better reflect practice, we introduce a realistic dynamic-environment threat model in which the attacker is a regular user and the trigger is embedded within a dynamically changing environment. Under this threat model, existing approaches largely fail, suggesting that their effectiveness in exposing GUI agent vulnerabilities has been substantially overestimated.\n To expose the hidden vulnerabilities of existing GUI agents effectively, we propose Chameleon, an attack framework with two key novelties designed for dynamic environments. (1) To synthesize more realistic training data, we introduce LLM-Driven Environment Simulation, which automatically generates diverse, high-fidelity webpage simulations that mimic the variability of real-world dynamic environments. (2) To optimize the trigger more effectively, we introduce Attention Black Hole, which converts attention weights into explicit supervisory signals. This mechanism encourages the agent to remain insensitive to irrelevant surrounding content, thereby improving robustness in dynamic environments. We evaluate Chameleon on six realistic websites and four representative LVLM-powered GUI agents, where it significantly outperforms existing methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11250","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:58:15Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:58:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:49:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11259","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11259","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11259","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Schiff","name":"Schiff, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ofir","familyName":"Lindenbaum","name":"Lindenbaum, Ofir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonathan","familyName":"Efroni","name":"Efroni, Yonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ICR-RL: Deep Reinforcement Learning via In-Context Regression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T13:09:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:44:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-06T06:45:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-07T02:02:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in machine learning have largely been driven by foundation models (FMs) trained on large, diverse datasets, enabling them to generalize effectively to new, related tasks. However, extending this paradigm to reinforcement learning (RL), where an agent interacts with an environment to select actions, remains a significant challenge. Most existing approaches train FMs directly on sets of control tasks, but developing diverse RL environments and scaling training across them can be costly and complex. In this study, we explore a simpler alternative approach based on a classical reduction from RL to regression. We demonstrate that a foundation model pre-trained for regression tasks, when used as an in-context regression (ICR) model, can be directly applied to RL problems. Building on this insight, we introduce a gradient-free method, ICR-RL, that requires no additional training and leverages an ICR foundation model to tackle RL tasks. We evaluate our approach by applying the ICR model with the recently proposed TabPFN, which is trained on a wide range of regression tasks. Experiments conducted on the Gymnasium classic-control benchmark indicate that ICR-RL can compete with commonly used methods, including DQN, PPO and TRPO. These results show that ICR foundation models can effectively solve RL tasks without fine-tuning, demonstrating their potential as a foundation for RL-oriented models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICML 2026 Main Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11259","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:58:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:58:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T04:57:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11292","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11292","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11292","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziling","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ziling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingqi","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Mingqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leveraging Geometric Priors for Unaligned Scene Change Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T14:31:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:45:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T06:25:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:26:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unaligned Scene Change Detection aims to detect scene changes between image pairs captured at different times without assuming viewpoint alignment. To handle viewpoint variations, current methods rely solely on 2D visual cues to establish cross-image correspondence to assist change detection. However, large viewpoint changes can alter visual observations, causing appearance-based matching to drift or fail. Additionally, supervision limited to 2D change masks from small-scale SCD datasets restricts the learning of generalizable multi-view knowledge, making it difficult to reliably identify visual overlaps and handle occlusions. This lack of explicit geometric reasoning represents a critical yet overlooked limitation. In this work, we introduce geometric priors for the first time to address the core challenges of unaligned SCD, for reliable identification of visual overlaps, robust correspondence establishment, and explicit occlusion detection. Building on these priors, we propose a training-free framework that integrates them with the powerful representations of a visual foundation model to enable reliable change detection under viewpoint misalignment. Through extensive evaluation on the PSCD, ChangeSim, and PASLCD datasets, we demonstrate that our approach achieves superior and robust performance. Our code will be released at https://github.com/ZilingLiu/GeoSCD."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11292","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:59:14Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:59:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:12:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11295","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11295","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11295","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"Romanov","name":"Romanov, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven A","familyName":"Niederer","name":"Niederer, Steven A","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Prompt Engineering Report Distilled: Quick Start Guide for Life Sciences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T14:39:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:46:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T13:18:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:48:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Developing effective prompts demands significant cognitive investment to generate reliable, high-quality responses from Large Language Models (LLMs). By deploying case-specific prompt engineering techniques that streamline frequently performed life sciences workflows, researchers could achieve substantial efficiency gains that far exceed the initial time investment required to master these techniques. The Prompt Report published in 2025 outlined 58 different text-based prompt engineering techniques, highlighting the numerous ways prompts could be constructed. To provide actionable guidelines and reduce the friction of navigating these various approaches, we distil this report to focus on 6 core techniques: zero-shot, few-shot approaches, thought generation, ensembling, self-criticism, and decomposition. We breakdown the significance of each approach and ground it in use cases relevant to life sciences, from literature summarization and data extraction to editorial tasks. We provide detailed recommendations for how prompts should and shouldn't be structured, addressing common pitfalls including multi-turn conversation degradation, hallucinations, and distinctions between reasoning and non-reasoning models. We examine context window limitations, agentic tools like Claude Code, while analyzing the effectiveness of Deep Research tools across OpenAI, Google, Anthropic and Perplexity platforms, discussing current limitations. We demonstrate how prompt engineering can augment rather than replace existing established individual practices around data processing and document editing. Our aim is to provide actionable guidance on core prompt engineering principles, and to facilitate the transition from opportunistic prompting to an effective, low-friction systematic practice that contributes to higher quality research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11295","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:59:18Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:59:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:19:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dasol","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Dasol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jungwhan","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jungwhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guijin","familyName":"Son","name":"Son, Guijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ko-PIQA: A Korean Physical Commonsense Reasoning Dataset with Cultural Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T14:47:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:46:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T06:44:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:27:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T01:32:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:26:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Physical commonsense reasoning datasets like PIQA are predominantly English-centric and lack cultural diversity. We introduce Ko-PIQA, a Korean physical commonsense reasoning dataset that incorporates cultural context. Starting from 3.01 million web-crawled questions, we employed a multi-stage filtering approach using three language models to identify 11,553 PIQA-style questions. Through GPT-4o refinement and human validation, we obtained 441 high-quality question-answer pairs. A key feature of Ko-PIQA is its cultural grounding: 19.7% of questions contain culturally specific elements like traditional Korean foods (kimchi), clothing (hanbok), and specialized appliances (kimchi refrigerators) that require culturally-aware reasoning beyond direct translation. We evaluate seven language models on Ko-PIQA, with the best model achieving 83.22% accuracy while the weakest reaches only 59.86%, demonstrating significant room for improvement. Models particularly struggle with culturally specific scenarios, highlighting the importance of culturally diverse datasets. Ko-PIQA serves as both a benchmark for Korean language models and a foundation for more inclusive commonsense reasoning research. The dataset and code will be publicly available."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:59:29Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:59:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:17:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingchen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zi-Yu","familyName":"Khoo","name":"Khoo, Zi-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingtan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingtan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompts to Proxies: Emulating Human Preferences via a Compact LLM Ensemble"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T15:08:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:46:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-28T19:39:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:04:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are increasingly used as proxies for human subjects in social science research, yet external validity requires that synthetic agents faithfully reflect the preferences of target human populations. We introduce *preference reconstruction theory*, a framework that formalizes preference alignment as a representation learning problem: constructing a functional basis of proxy agents and recovering population preferences through weighted aggregation. We implement this via *Prompts to Proxies* ($\\texttt{P2P}$), a modular two-stage system. Stage 1 uses structured prompting with entropy-based adaptive sampling to construct a diverse agent pool spanning the latent preference space. Stage 2 employs L1-regularized regression to select a compact ensemble whose aggregate response distributions align with observed data from the target population. $\\texttt{P2P}$ requires no finetuning and no access to sensitive demographic data, incurring only API inference costs. We validate the approach on 14 waves of the American Trends Panel, achieving an average test MSE of 0.014 across diverse topics at approximately 0.8 USD per survey. We additionally test it on the World Values Survey, demonstrating its potential to generalize across locales. When stress-tested against an SFT-aligned baseline, $\\texttt{P2P}$ achieves competitive performance using less than 3% of the training data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:59:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:59:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-30T03:41:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11353","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11353","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11353","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanpei","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Hanpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijie","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Sijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Nuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai-Xin","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Kai-Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tetsuya","familyName":"Sakai","name":"Sakai, Tetsuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do Large Language Models Favor Recent Content? A Study on Recency Bias in LLM-Based Reranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T17:11:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:49:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-21T16:59:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T02:16:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in information systems, including being used as second-stage rerankers in information retrieval pipelines, yet their susceptibility to recency bias has received little attention. We investigate whether LLMs implicitly favour newer documents by prepending artificial publication dates to passages in the TREC Deep Learning passage retrieval collections in 2021 (DL21) and 2022 (DL22). Across seven models, GPT-3.5-turbo, GPT-4o, GPT-4, LLaMA-3 8B/70B, and Qwen-2.5 7B/72B, \"fresh\" passages are consistently promoted, shifting the Top-10's mean publication year forward by up to 4.78 years and moving individual items by as many as 95 ranks in our listwise reranking experiments. Although larger models attenuate the effect, none eliminate it. We also observe that the preference of LLMs between two passages with an identical relevance level can be reversed by up to 25% on average after date injection in our pairwise preference experiments. These findings provide quantitative evidence of a pervasive recency bias in LLMs and highlight the importance of effective bias-mitigation strategies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11353","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:00:43Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:00:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:42:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11361","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11361","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11361","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siteng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Siteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Guanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengpeng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhengpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bojun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujia","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isaac N","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Isaac N","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lewei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Lewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MAPGD: Multi-Agent Prompt Gradient Descent for Collaborative Prompt Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T17:28:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:49:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T09:25:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:35:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T09:31:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:46:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prompt engineering is crucial for fully leveraging large language models (LLMs), yet most existing optimization methods follow a single trajectory, resulting in limited adaptability, gradient conflicts, and high computational overhead. We propose MAPGD (Multi-Agent Prompt Gradient Descent), a novel framework that reconceptualizes prompt optimization as a collaborative process among specialized agents. Each agent focuses on a distinct refinement dimension, such as instruction clarity, example selection, format structure, or stylistic adaptation, and their contributions are coordinated through semantic gradient embedding, conflict detection, and fusion. To further enhance robustness and stability, MAPGD introduces two new mechanisms: Hypersphere Constrained Gradient Clustering (HCGC), which enforces angular margin constraints for compact and well-separated clusters, and Channel Adaptive Agent Weighting (CAAW), which dynamically reweights agent contributions based on validation performance. Experiments on classification and reasoning benchmarks show that MAPGD consistently surpasses single-agent and random baselines in both accuracy and efficiency. Ablation studies confirm the effectiveness of gradient fusion, agent specialization, and conflict resolution. Together, these components establish MAPGD as a unified, gradient-based, and interpretable framework for robust prompt optimization with theoretical convergence guarantees."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11361","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:00:54Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:00:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-04T03:40:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11362","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11362","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11362","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Loka","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Loka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wong Yu","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Wong Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gongxu","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Gongxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuewen","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuewen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salman","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Salman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Spirtes","name":"Spirtes, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PersonaX: Multimodal Datasets with LLM-Inferred Behavior Traits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T17:30:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:49:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-10T12:33:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-11T01:48:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding human behavior traits is central to applications in human-computer interaction, computational social science, and personalized AI systems. Such understanding often requires integrating multiple modalities to capture nuanced patterns and relationships. However, existing resources rarely provide datasets that combine behavioral descriptors with complementary modalities such as facial attributes and biographical information. To address this gap, we present PersonaX, a curated collection of multimodal datasets designed to enable comprehensive analysis of public traits across modalities. PersonaX consists of (1) CelebPersona, featuring 9444 public figures from diverse occupations, and (2) AthlePersona, covering 4181 professional athletes across 7 major sports leagues. Each dataset includes behavioral trait assessments inferred by three high-performing large language models, alongside facial imagery and structured biographical features. We analyze PersonaX at two complementary levels. First, we abstract high-level trait scores from text descriptions and apply five statistical independence tests to examine their relationships with other modalities. Second, we introduce a novel causal representation learning (CRL) framework tailored to multimodal and multi-measurement data, providing theoretical identifiability guarantees. Experiments on both synthetic and real-world data demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach. By unifying structured and unstructured analysis, PersonaX establishes a foundation for studying LLM-inferred behavioral traits in conjunction with visual and biographical attributes, advancing multimodal trait analysis and causal reasoning. The code is available at https://github.com/lokali/PersonaX."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11362","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:00:55Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:00:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-11T03:28:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11376","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11376","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11376","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyed Kourosh","familyName":"Mahjour","name":"Mahjour, Seyed Kourosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyed Saman","familyName":"Mahjour","name":"Mahjour, Seyed Saman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Intelligent Reservoir Decision Support: An Integrated Framework Combining Large Language Models, Advanced Prompt Engineering, and Multimodal Data Fusion for Real-Time Petroleum Operations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T18:13:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:50:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The petroleum industry faces unprecedented challenges in reservoir management, requiring rapid integration of complex multimodal datasets for real-time decision support. This study presents a novel integrated framework combining state-of-the-art large language models (GPT-4o, Claude 4 Sonnet, Gemini 2.5 Pro) with advanced prompt engineering techniques and multimodal data fusion for comprehensive reservoir analysis. The framework implements domain-specific retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) with over 50,000 petroleum engineering documents, chain-of-thought reasoning, and few-shot learning for rapid field adaptation. Multimodal integration processes seismic interpretations, well logs, and production data through specialized AI models with vision transformers. Field validation across 15 diverse reservoir environments demonstrates exceptional performance: 94.2% reservoir characterization accuracy, 87.6% production forecasting precision, and 91.4% well placement optimization success rate. The system achieves sub-second response times while maintaining 96.2% safety reliability with no high-risk incidents during evaluation. Economic analysis reveals 62-78% cost reductions (mean 72%) relative to traditional methods with 8-month payback period. Few-shot learning reduces field adaptation time by 72%, while automated prompt optimization achieves 89% improvement in reasoning quality. The framework processed real-time data streams with 96.2% anomaly detection accuracy and reduced environmental incidents by 45%. We provide detailed experimental protocols, baseline comparisons, ablation studies, and statistical significance testing to ensure reproducibility. This research demonstrates practical integration of cutting-edge AI technologies with petroleum domain expertise for enhanced operational efficiency, safety, and economic performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11376","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:01:15Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:01:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:01:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11417","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11417","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11417","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shresth","familyName":"Grover","name":"Grover, Shresth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshay","familyName":"Gopalkrishnan","name":"Gopalkrishnan, Akshay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henrik I.","familyName":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Henrik I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuanlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Generalization in Vision-Language-Action Models by Preserving Pretrained Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T20:08:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:52:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T02:41:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:18:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language-action (VLA) models finetuned from vision-language models (VLMs) hold the promise of leveraging rich pretrained representations to build generalist robots across diverse tasks and environments. However, direct fine-tuning on robot data often disrupts these representations and limits generalization. We present a framework that better preserves pretrained features while adapting them for robot manipulation. Our approach introduces three components: (i) a dual-encoder design with one frozen vision encoder to retain pretrained features and another trainable for task adaptation, (ii) a string-based action tokenizer that casts continuous actions into character sequences aligned with the model's pretraining domain, and (iii) a co-training strategy that combines robot demonstrations with vision-language datasets emphasizing spatial reasoning and affordances. Evaluations in simulation and on real robots show that our method improves robustness to visual perturbations, generalization to novel instructions and environments, and overall task success compared to baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://gen-vla.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11417","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:02:12Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:02:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:44:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11420","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11420","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11420","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijia","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Edward","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trading-R1: Financial Trading with LLM Reasoning via Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Trading and Market Microstructure (q-fin.TR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T20:13:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:52:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Developing professional, structured reasoning on par with human financial analysts and traders remains a central challenge in AI for finance, where markets demand interpretability and trust. Traditional time-series models lack explainability, while LLMs face challenges in turning natural-language analysis into disciplined, executable trades. Although reasoning LLMs have advanced in step-by-step planning and verification, their application to risk-sensitive financial decisions is underexplored. We present Trading-R1, a financially-aware model that incorporates strategic thinking and planning for comprehensive thesis composition, facts-grounded analysis, and volatility-adjusted decision making. Trading-R1 aligns reasoning with trading principles through supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning with a three-stage easy-to-hard curriculum. Training uses Tauric-TR1-DB, a 100k-sample corpus spanning 18 months, 14 equities, and five heterogeneous financial data sources. Evaluated on six major equities and ETFs, Trading-R1 demonstrates improved risk-adjusted returns and lower drawdowns compared to both open-source and proprietary instruction-following models as well as reasoning models. The system generates structured, evidence-based investment theses that support disciplined and interpretable trading decisions. Trading-R1 Terminal will be released at https://github.com/TauricResearch/Trading-R1."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Tauric Research: https://github.com/TauricResearch"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11420","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:02:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:02:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:02:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11431","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11431","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11431","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aadil Gani","familyName":"Ganie","name":"Ganie, Aadil Gani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Securing AI Agents: Implementing Role-Based Access Control for Industrial Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T20:58:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:53:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of Large Language Models (LLMs) has significantly advanced solutions across various domains, from political science to software development. However, these models are constrained by their training data, which is static and limited to information available up to a specific date. Additionally, their generalized nature often necessitates fine-tuning -- whether for classification or instructional purposes -- to effectively perform specific downstream tasks. AI agents, leveraging LLMs as their core, mitigate some of these limitations by accessing external tools and real-time data, enabling applications such as live weather reporting and data analysis. In industrial settings, AI agents are transforming operations by enhancing decision-making, predictive maintenance, and process optimization. For example, in manufacturing, AI agents enable near-autonomous systems that boost productivity and support real-time decision-making. Despite these advancements, AI agents remain vulnerable to security threats, including prompt injection attacks, which pose significant risks to their integrity and reliability. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a framework for integrating Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) into AI agents, providing a robust security guardrail. This framework aims to support the effective and scalable deployment of AI agents, with a focus on on-premises implementations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11431","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:02:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:02:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:02:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11447","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11447","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11447","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiachen","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jiachen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanwook","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Chanwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas J. R.","familyName":"Hughes","name":"Hughes, Thomas J. R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wing Kam","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wing Kam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large language model-empowered next-generation computer-aided engineering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T21:45:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:54:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Software development has entered a new era where large language models (LLMs) now serve as general-purpose reasoning engines, enabling natural language interaction and transformative applications across diverse domains. This paradigm is now extending into computer-aided engineering (CAE). Recent applications of LLMs in CAE have successfully automated routine tasks, including CAD model generation and FEM simulations. Nevertheless, these contributions, which primarily serve to reduce manual labor, are often insufficient for addressing the significant computational challenges posed by large-scale, high-dimensional systems. To this aim, we first introduce the concept of LLM-empowered CAE agent, where LLMs act as autonomous collaborators that plan, execute, and adapt CAE workflows. Then, we propose an LLM-empowered CAE agent for data-free model order reduction (MOR), a powerful yet underused approach for ultra-fast large-scale parametric analysis due to the intrusive nature and labor-intensive redevelopment of solvers. LLMs can alleviate this barrier by automating derivations, code restructuring, and implementation, making intrusive MOR both practical and broadly accessible. To demonstrate feasibility, we present an LLM-empowered CAE agent for solving ultra-large-scale space-parameter-time (S-P-T) physical problems using Tensor-decomposition-based A Priori Surrogates (TAPS). Our results show that natural language prompts describing parametric partial differential equations (PDEs) can be translated into efficient solver implementations, substantially reducing human effort while producing high-fidelity reduced-order models. Moreover, LLMs can synthesize novel MOR solvers for unseen cases such as nonlinear and high-dimensional parametric problems based on their internal knowledge base. This highlights the potential of LLMs to establish the foundation for next-generation CAE systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11447","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:02:53Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:02:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:02:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11465","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11465","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11465","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Abaskohi","name":"Abaskohi, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raymond","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Raymond","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chuyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shafiq","familyName":"Joty","name":"Joty, Shafiq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Carenini","name":"Carenini, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CEMTM: Contextual Embedding-based Multimodal Topic Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T23:07:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:55:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-05T16:11:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:00:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce CEMTM, a context-enhanced multimodal topic model designed to infer coherent and interpretable topic structures from both short and long documents containing text and images. CEMTM builds on fine-tuned large vision language models (LVLMs) to obtain contextualized embeddings, and employs a distributional attention mechanism to weight token-level contributions to topic inference. A reconstruction objective aligns topic-based representations with the document embedding, encouraging semantic consistency across modalities. Unlike existing approaches, CEMTM can process multiple images per document without repeated encoding and maintains interpretability through explicit word-topic and document-topic distributions. Extensive experiments on six multimodal benchmarks show that CEMTM consistently outperforms unimodal and multimodal baselines, achieving a remarkable average LLM score of 2.61. Further analysis shows its effectiveness in downstream few-shot retrieval and its ability to capture visually grounded semantics in complex domains such as scientific articles."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11465","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:19Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:49:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11478","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11478","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11478","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew G.","familyName":"Breithaupt","name":"Breithaupt, Andrew G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nayoung","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Nayoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James D.","familyName":"Finch","name":"Finch, James D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeanne M.","familyName":"Powell","name":"Powell, Jeanne M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arin L.","familyName":"Nelson","name":"Nelson, Arin L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oz A.","familyName":"Alon","name":"Alon, Oz A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Howard J.","familyName":"Rosen","name":"Rosen, Howard J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinho D.","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Jinho D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Designing and Evaluating a Conversational Agent for Early Diagnosis of Alzheimer's Disease and Related Dementias"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T23:55:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:56:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T15:50:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T02:01:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Early diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease and related dementias (ADRD) is critical for timely intervention, yet most diagnoses are delayed until advanced stages. While comprehensive patient narratives are essential for accurate diagnosis, prior work has largely focused on screening studies that classify cognitive status from interactions rather than supporting the diagnostic process. We designed voice-interactive conversational agents, leveraging large language models (LLMs), to elicit narratives relevant to ADRD from patients and informants. We evaluated the agent with 30 adults with suspected ADRD through conversation analysis, user surveys, and analysis of symptom elicitation compared to blinded specialist interviews. Symptoms detected by the agent showed promising agreement with those identified by specialists. Users appreciated the agent's patience and systematic questioning, which supported engagement and expression of complex, hard-to-describe experiences. While these findings suggest potential for conversational agents as structured diagnostic support tools, further validation with larger samples and assessment of clinical utility is needed before deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"First two authors contributed equally"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11478","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:37Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-28T03:23:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11480","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11480","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11480","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Taherin","name":"Taherin, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juyi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Juyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Akbari","name":"Akbari, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Akbari","name":"Akbari, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kaeli","name":"Kaeli, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Platform Scaling of Vision-Language-Action Models from Edge to Cloud GPUs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:56:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T15:57:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:36:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have emerged as powerful generalist policies for robotic control, yet their performance scaling across model architectures and hardware platforms, as well as their associated power budgets, remain poorly understood. This work presents an evaluation of five representative VLA models -- spanning state-of-the-art baselines and two newly proposed architectures -- targeting edge and datacenter GPU platforms. Using the LIBERO benchmark, we measure accuracy alongside system-level metrics, including latency, throughput, and peak memory usage, under varying edge power constraints and high-performance datacenter GPU configurations. Our results identify distinct scaling trends: (1) architectural choices, such as action tokenization and model backbone size, strongly influence throughput and memory footprint; (2) power-constrained edge devices exhibit non-linear performance degradation, with some configurations matching or exceeding older datacenter GPUs; and (3) high-throughput variants can be achieved without significant accuracy loss. These findings provide actionable insights when selecting and optimizing VLAs across a range of deployment constraints. Our work challenges current assumptions about the superiority of datacenter hardware for robotic inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in the Asilomar Conference on Signals, Systems, and Computers 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11480","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:34:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11507","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11507","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11507","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jared","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jared","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junde","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Junde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedicalOS: An LLM Agent based Operating System for Digital Healthcare"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T01:43:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:57:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Decades' advances in digital health technologies, such as electronic health records, have largely streamlined routine clinical processes. Yet, most these systems are still hard to learn and use: Clinicians often face the burden of managing multiple tools, repeating manual actions for each patient, navigating complicated UI trees to locate functions, and spending significant time on administration instead of caring for patients. The recent rise of large language model (LLM) based agents demonstrates exceptional capability in coding and computer operation, revealing the potential for humans to interact with operating systems and software not by direct manipulation, but by instructing agents through natural language. This shift highlights the need for an abstraction layer, an agent-computer interface, that translates human language into machine-executable commands. In digital healthcare, however, requires a more domain-specific abstractions that strictly follow trusted clinical guidelines and procedural standards to ensure safety, transparency, and compliance. To address this need, we present \\textbf{MedicalOS}, a unified agent-based operational system designed as such a domain-specific abstract layer for healthcare. It translates human instructions into pre-defined digital healthcare commands, such as patient inquiry, history retrieval, exam management, report generation, referrals, treatment planning, that we wrapped as off-the-shelf tools using machine languages (e.g., Python, APIs, MCP, Linux). We empirically validate MedicalOS on 214 patient cases across 22 specialties, demonstrating high diagnostic accuracy and confidence, clinically sound examination requests, and consistent generation of structured reports and medication recommendations. These results highlight MedicalOS as a trustworthy and scalable foundation for advancing workflow automation in clinical practice."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11507","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:04:18Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:04:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:04:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiling","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weiling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panagiotis","familyName":"Kaliosis","name":"Kaliosis, Panagiotis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Owen","familyName":"Rambow","name":"Rambow, Owen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susan E.","familyName":"Brennan","name":"Brennan, Susan E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LVLMs are Bad at Overhearing Human Referential Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T02:03:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:58:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:25:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:13:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"During spontaneous conversations, speakers collaborate on novel referring expressions, which they can then re-use in subsequent conversations. Understanding such referring expressions is an important ability for an embodied agent, so that it can carry out tasks in the real world. This requires integrating and understanding language, vision, and conversational interaction. We study the capabilities of seven state-of-the-art Large Vision Language Models (LVLMs) as overhearers to a corpus of spontaneous conversations between pairs of human discourse participants engaged in a collaborative object-matching task. We find that such a task remains challenging for current LVLMs and they all fail to show a consistent performance improvement as they overhear more conversations from the same discourse participants repeating the same task for multiple rounds. We release our corpus and code for reproducibility and to facilitate future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 (Main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:04:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:04:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:11:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11523","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11523","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11523","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VulAgent: Hypothesis-Validation based Multi-Agent Vulnerability Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T02:25:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:58:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The application of language models to project-level vulnerability detection remains challenging, owing to the dual requirement of accurately localizing security-sensitive code and correctly correlating and reasoning over complex program context. We present VulAgent, a multi-agent vulnerability detection framework based on hypothesis validation. Our design is inspired by how human auditors review code: when noticing a sensitive operation, they form a hypothesis about a possible vulnerability, consider potential trigger paths, and then verify the hypothesis against the surrounding context. VulAgent implements a semantics-sensitive, multi-view detection pipeline: specialized agents, each aligned to a specific analysis perspective (e.g., memory, authorization), collaboratively surface and precisely localize sensitive code sites with higher coverage. Building on this, VulAgent adopts a hypothesis-validation paradigm: for each vulnerability report, it builds hypothesis conditions and a trigger path, steering the LLM to target the relevant program context and defensive checks during verification, which reduces false positives. On average across the two datasets, VulAgent improves overall accuracy by 6.6%, increases the correct identification rate of vulnerable--fixed code pairs by up to 450% (246% on average), and reduces the false positive rate by about 36% compared with state-of-the-art LLM-based baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11523","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:04:41Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:04:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:04:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"ShengYu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, ShengYu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinxin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinting","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhilong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongbo","familyName":"Shan","name":"Shan, Dongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HARP: Hallucination Detection via Reasoning Subspace Projection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T03:02:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:59:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-05T07:28:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-02T00:19:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hallucinations in Large Language Models (LLMs) pose a major barrier to their reliable use in critical decision-making. Although existing hallucination detection methods have improved accuracy, they still struggle with disentangling semantic and reasoning information and maintaining robustness. To address these challenges, we propose HARP (Hallucination detection via reasoning subspace projection), a novel hallucination detection framework. HARP establishes that the hidden state space of LLMs can be decomposed into a direct sum of a semantic subspace and a reasoning subspace, where the former encodes linguistic expression and the latter captures internal reasoning processes. Moreover, we demonstrate that the Unembedding layer can disentangle these subspaces, and by applying Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) to its parameters, the basis vectors spanning the semantic and reasoning subspaces are obtained. Finally, HARP projects hidden states onto the basis vectors of the reasoning subspace, and the resulting projections are then used as input features for hallucination detection in LLMs. By using these projections, HARP reduces the dimension of the feature to approximately 5% of the original, filters out most noise, and achieves enhanced robustness. Experiments across multiple datasets show that HARP achieves state-of-the-art hallucination detection performance; in particular, it achieves an AUROC of 92.8% on TriviaQA, outperforming the previous best method by 7.5%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:04:59Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:04:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-02T02:27:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11548","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11548","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11548","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yujing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongxiao","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Tongxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manni","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Manni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Auxiliary Reasoning Unleashes GUI Grounding in VLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T03:28:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:00:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-10T01:13:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:19:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graphical user interface (GUI) grounding is a fundamental task for building GUI agents. However, general vision-language models (VLMs) struggle with this task due to a lack of specific optimization. We identify a key gap in this paper: while VLMs exhibit significant latent grounding potential, as demonstrated by their performance measured by Pointing Game, they underperform when tasked with outputting explicit coordinates. To address this discrepancy and bypass the high data and annotation costs of current fine-tuning approaches, we propose three zero-shot auxiliary reasoning methods. By providing explicit spatial cues such as axes, grids and labeled intersections as part of the input image, these methods enable VLMs to better articulate their implicit spatial understanding capabilities. We evaluate these methods on four GUI grounding benchmarks across seven open-source and proprietary VLMs. Experimental results show substantial gains from auxiliary reasoning. Mark-Grid Scaffold boosts Gemini-3.1-Pro from 11.72\\% under direct inference to 95.20\\% on ScreenSpot-v2, achieves state-of-the-art performance on ScreenSpot, and approaches the strongest fine-tuned methods on ScreenSpot-v2 and UI-I2E-Bench. Our code is available at https://github.com/liweim/AuxiliaryReasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11548","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:05:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:05:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-11T02:24:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11552","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11552","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11552","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wensheng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Wensheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Keyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruizhi","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Ruizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HiChunk: Evaluating and Enhancing Retrieval-Augmented Generation with Hierarchical Chunking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T03:32:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:00:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T12:36:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:44:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T14:21:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:59:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enhances the response capabilities of language models by integrating external knowledge sources. However, document chunking as an important part of RAG system often lacks effective evaluation tools. This paper first analyzes why existing RAG evaluation benchmarks are inadequate for assessing document chunking quality, specifically due to evidence sparsity. Based on this conclusion, we propose HiCBench, which includes manually annotated multi-level document chunking points, synthesized evidence-dense quetion answer(QA) pairs, and their corresponding evidence sources. Additionally, we introduce the HiChunk framework, a multi-level document structuring framework based on fine-tuned LLMs, combined with the Auto-Merge retrieval algorithm to improve retrieval quality. Experiments demonstrate that HiCBench effectively evaluates the impact of different chunking methods across the entire RAG pipeline. Moreover, HiChunk achieves better chunking quality within reasonable time consumption, thereby enhancing the overall performance of RAG systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 5 figures, 6 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11552","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:05:22Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:05:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:37:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11569","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11569","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11569","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xiaofang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianze","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Tianze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Junfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinlong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xinlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"D$^2$HScore: Reasoning-Aware Hallucination Detection via Semantic Breadth and Depth Analysis in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T04:28:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:01:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success, their practical application is often hindered by the generation of non-factual content, which is called \"hallucination\". Ensuring the reliability of LLMs' outputs is a critical challenge, particularly in high-stakes domains such as finance, security, and healthcare. In this work, we revisit hallucination detection from the perspective of model architecture and generation dynamics. Leveraging the multi-layer structure and autoregressive decoding process of LLMs, we decompose hallucination signals into two complementary dimensions: the semantic breadth of token representations within each layer, and the semantic depth of core concepts as they evolve across layers. Based on this insight, we propose \\textbf{D$^2$HScore (Dispersion and Drift-based Hallucination Score)}, a training-free and label-free framework that jointly measures: (1) \\textbf{Intra-Layer Dispersion}, which quantifies the semantic diversity of token representations within each layer; and (2) \\textbf{Inter-Layer Drift}, which tracks the progressive transformation of key token representations across layers. To ensure drift reflects the evolution of meaningful semantics rather than noisy or redundant tokens, we guide token selection using attention signals. By capturing both the horizontal and vertical dynamics of representation during inference, D$^2$HScore provides an interpretable and lightweight proxy for hallucination detection. Extensive experiments across five open-source LLMs and five widely used benchmarks demonstrate that D$^2$HScore consistently outperforms existing training-free baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11569","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:05:46Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:05:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:05:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11572","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11572","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11572","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuan","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Tuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phat","familyName":"Thai","name":"Thai, Phat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Minh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngan Pham L.","familyName":"N.","name":"N., Ngan Pham L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngan Pham T.","familyName":"B.","name":"B., Ngan Pham T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dung","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Dung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh-Tung","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Thanh-Tung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thang","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Thang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tho","familyName":"Quan","name":"Quan, Tho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Formal Reasoning for Intelligent QA Systems: A Case Study in the Educational Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T04:34:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:02:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746274.3760392","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning is essential for closed-domain QA systems in which procedural correctness and policy compliance are critical. While large language models (LLMs) have shown strong performance on many reasoning tasks, recent work reveals that their reasoning traces are often unfaithful - serving more as plausible justifications than as causally grounded derivations. Efforts to combine LLMs with symbolic engines (e.g., Prover9, Z3) have improved reliability but remain limited to static forms of logic, struggling with dynamic, state-based reasoning such as multi-step progressions and conditional transitions.\n In this paper, we propose MCFR (Model Checking for Formal Reasoning), a neuro-symbolic framework that integrates LLMs with model checking to support property verification. MCFR translates natural language into formal specifications and verifies them over transition models. To support evaluation, we introduce EduMC-QA, a benchmark dataset grounded in real academic procedures. Our results show that MCFR improves reasoning faithfulness and interpretability, offering a viable path toward verifiable QA in high-stakes closed-domain applications. In addition to evaluating MCFR, we compare its performance with state-of-the-art LLMs such as ChatGPT, DeepSeek, and Claude to contextualize its effectiveness."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at the 2nd ACM Workshop in AI-powered Question \u0026amp; Answering Systems (AIQAM '25), co-located with ACM Multimedia 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11572","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:05:50Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:05:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:05:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11575","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11575","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11575","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ching","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Ching","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yidan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Defu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Defu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeehyun","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Jeehyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haixin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen-Chih","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Wen-Chih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tien-Fu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tien-Fu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey of Reasoning and Agentic Systems in Time Series with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T04:39:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-02T22:14:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:07:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-10T07:42:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:35:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Time series reasoning treats time as a first-class axis and incorporates intermediate evidence directly into the answer. This survey defines the problem and organizes the literature by reasoning topology with three families: direct reasoning in one step, linear chain reasoning with explicit intermediates, and branch-structured reasoning that explores, revises, and aggregates. The topology is crossed with the main objectives of the field, including traditional time series analysis, explanation and understanding, causal inference and decision making, and time series generation, while a compact tag set spans these axes and captures decomposition and verification, ensembling, tool use, knowledge access, multimodality, agent loops, and LLM alignment regimes. Methods and systems are reviewed across domains, showing what each topology enables and where it breaks down in faithfulness or robustness, along with curated datasets, benchmarks, and resources that support study and deployment (https://github.com/blacksnail789521/Time-Series-Reasoning-Survey). Evaluation practices that keep evidence visible and temporally aligned are highlighted, and guidance is distilled on matching topology to uncertainty, grounding with observable artifacts, planning for shift and streaming, and treating cost and latency as design budgets. We emphasize that reasoning structures must balance capacity for grounding and self-correction against computational cost and reproducibility, while future progress will likely depend on benchmarks that tie reasoning quality to utility and on closed-loop testbeds that trade off cost and risk under shift-aware, streaming, and long-horizon settings. Taken together, these directions mark a shift from narrow accuracy toward reliability at scale, enabling systems that not only analyze but also understand, explain, and act on dynamic worlds with traceable evidence and credible outcomes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research (TMLR)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11575","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:05:54Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:05:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-11T02:24:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11589","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11589","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11589","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyun","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Yanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kehan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zeyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijie","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Zhijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixiang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kaixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MVQA-68K: A Multi-dimensional and Causally-annotated Dataset with Quality Interpretability for Video Assessment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T05:16:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:03:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of video generation models such as Sora, video quality assessment (VQA) is becoming increasingly crucial for selecting high-quality videos from large-scale datasets used in pre-training. Traditional VQA methods, typically producing single numerical scores, often lack comprehensiveness and interpretability. To address these challenges, we introduce MVQA-68K, a novel multi-dimensional VQA dataset comprising over 68,000 carefully annotated videos, covering seven essential quality dimensions: overall aesthetics, camera movement, dynamic degree, texture detail, composition, visual quality, and factual consistency. Each annotation includes detailed chain-of-thought reasoning to facilitate interpretability and comprehensive understanding. Extensive experiments demonstrate that MVQA-68K significantly enhances the performance of various multimodal large language models (MLLMs) on the VQA task, achieving state-of-the-art results not only on our internal test set (Fig.1) but also on public benchmarks including LSVQ-test, LSVQ-1080p, and LIVE-VQC. Meantime, incorporating explicit reasoning process during VQA training substantially boosts the zero-shot generalization. Code and dataset will be available at github: https://github.com/Controller01-ai/MVQA-68K"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11589","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:06:13Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:06:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:06:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11617","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11617","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11617","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoran","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chaoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"EnTe","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, EnTe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubo","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Shubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghongbing","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Qinghongbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaobo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AssemMate: Graph-Based LLM for Robotic Assembly Assistance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T06:17:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:04:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T05:58:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM)-based robotic assembly assistance has gained significant research attention. It requires the injection of domain-specific knowledge to guide the assembly process through natural language interaction with humans. Despite some progress, existing methods represent knowledge in the form of natural language text. Due to the long context and redundant content, they struggle to meet the robots' requirements for real-time and precise reasoning. In order to bridge this gap, we present AssemMate, which utilizes the graph\\textemdash a concise and accurate form of knowledge representation\\textemdash as input. This graph-based LLM enables knowledge graph question answering (KGQA), supporting human-robot interaction and assembly task planning for specific products. Beyond interactive QA, AssemMate also supports sensing stacked scenes and executing grasping to assist with assembly. Specifically, a self-supervised Graph Convolutional Network (GCN) encodes knowledge graph entities and relations into a latent space and aligns them with LLM's representation, enabling the LLM to understand graph information. In addition, a vision-enhanced strategy is employed to address stacked scenes in grasping. Through training and evaluation, AssemMate outperforms existing methods, achieving 6.4\\% higher accuracy, 3 times faster inference, and 28 times shorter context length, while demonstrating strong generalization ability on random graphs. And our approach further demonstrates superiority through robotic grasping experiments in both simulated and real-world settings. More details can be found on the project page: https://github.com/cristina304/AssemMate.git"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11617","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:06:52Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:06:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:42:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11626","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11626","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11626","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prerna","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Prerna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Himanshu","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Himanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soujanya","familyName":"Soni","name":"Soni, Soujanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rohith","familyName":"Vallam","name":"Vallam, Rohith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renuka","familyName":"Sindhgatta","name":"Sindhgatta, Renuka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sameep","familyName":"Mehta","name":"Mehta, Sameep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated Creation and Enrichment Framework for Improved Invocation of Enterprise APIs as Tools"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T06:41:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:05:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) has lead to the development of agents capable of complex reasoning and interaction with external tools. In enterprise contexts, the effective use of such tools that are often enabled by application programming interfaces (APIs), is hindered by poor documentation, complex input or output schema, and large number of operations. These challenges make tool selection difficult and reduce the accuracy of payload formation by up to 25%. We propose ACE, an automated tool creation and enrichment framework that transforms enterprise APIs into LLM-compatible tools. ACE, (i) generates enriched tool specifications with parameter descriptions and examples to improve selection and invocation accuracy, and (ii) incorporates a dynamic shortlisting mechanism that filters relevant tools at runtime, reducing prompt complexity while maintaining scalability. We validate our framework on both proprietary and open-source APIs and demonstrate its integration with agentic frameworks. To the best of our knowledge, ACE is the first end-to-end framework that automates the creation, enrichment, and dynamic selection of enterprise API tools for LLM agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11626","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:07:05Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:07:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:07:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11629","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11629","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11629","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chentao","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Chentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoned Safety Alignment: Ensuring Jailbreak Defense via Answer-Then-Check"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T06:47:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:05:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-06T02:11:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:17:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) continue to advance in capabilities, ensuring their safety against jailbreak attacks remains a critical challenge. In this paper, we introduce a novel safety alignment approach called Answer-Then-Check, which enhances LLM robustness against malicious prompts by applying thinking ability to mitigate jailbreaking problems before producing a final answer to the user. Our method enables models to answer the question in their thoughts directly and then critically evaluate its safety before deciding whether to provide it. To implement this approach, we construct the Reasoned Safety Alignment (ReSA) dataset, comprising 80K samples that teach models to reason through direct responses and then analyze their safety. Experimental results demonstrate that our approach achieves the Pareto frontier with superior safety capability while decreasing over-refusal rates. Notably, the fine-tuned model maintains general reasoning capabilities on benchmarks like MMLU, MATH500, and HumanEval. Besides, our method equips models with the ability to perform safe completion, while post-hoc detection methods can only directly reject sensitive, harmful queries (e.g., self-harm). Our results show that inference-time strategies alone are insufficient, highlighting the necessity of safety training, and we find even $500$ samples can yield performance comparable to the entire dataset, suggesting a promising path for data-efficient safety alignment. The dataset is publicly available at: https://huggingface.co/datasets/ByteDance-Seed/ReSA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11629","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:07:09Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:07:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:08:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11645","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11645","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11645","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaolong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason Pui Yin","familyName":"Cheung","name":"Cheung, Jason Pui Yin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Teng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adapting and Evaluating Multimodal Large Language Models for Adolescent Idiopathic Scoliosis Self-Management: A Divide and Conquer Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T07:34:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:07:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T09:02:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:43:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-06004-4_28","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study presents the first comprehensive evaluation of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) for Adolescent Idiopathic Scoliosis (AIS) self-management. We constructed a database of approximately 3,000 anteroposterior X-rays with diagnostic texts and evaluated five MLLMs through a `Divide and Conquer' framework consisting of a visual question-answering task, a domain knowledge assessment task, and a patient education counseling assessment task. Our investigation revealed limitations of MLLMs' ability in interpreting complex spinal radiographs and comprehending AIS care knowledge. To address these, we pioneered enhancing MLLMs with spinal keypoint prompting and compiled an AIS knowledge base for retrieval augmented generation (RAG), respectively. Results showed varying effectiveness of visual prompting across different architectures, while RAG substantially improved models' performances on the knowledge assessment task. Our findings indicate current MLLMs are far from capable in realizing personalized assistant in AIS care. The greatest challenge lies in their abilities to obtain accurate detections of spinal deformity locations (best accuracy: 0.55) and directions (best accuracy: 0.13)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by MICCAI 2025 MLLMCP Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11645","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:07:36Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:07:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:21:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11648","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11648","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11648","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai Kartheek Reddy","familyName":"Kasu","name":"Kasu, Sai Kartheek Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EthicsMH: A Pilot Benchmark for Ethical Reasoning in Mental Health AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T07:35:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:07:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The deployment of large language models (LLMs) in mental health and other sensitive domains raises urgent questions about ethical reasoning, fairness, and responsible alignment. Yet, existing benchmarks for moral and clinical decision-making do not adequately capture the unique ethical dilemmas encountered in mental health practice, where confidentiality, autonomy, beneficence, and bias frequently intersect. To address this gap, we introduce Ethical Reasoning in Mental Health (EthicsMH), a pilot dataset of 125 scenarios designed to evaluate how AI systems navigate ethically charged situations in therapeutic and psychiatric contexts. Each scenario is enriched with structured fields, including multiple decision options, expert-aligned reasoning, expected model behavior, real-world impact, and multi-stakeholder viewpoints. This structure enables evaluation not only of decision accuracy but also of explanation quality and alignment with professional norms. Although modest in scale and developed with model-assisted generation, EthicsMH establishes a task framework that bridges AI ethics and mental health decision-making. By releasing this dataset, we aim to provide a seed resource that can be expanded through community and expert contributions, fostering the development of AI systems capable of responsibly handling some of society's most delicate decisions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11648","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:07:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:07:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:07:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11656","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11656","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11656","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Becker","name":"Becker, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars Benedikt","familyName":"Kaesberg","name":"Kaesberg, Lars Benedikt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niklas","familyName":"Bauer","name":"Bauer, Niklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan Philip","familyName":"Wahle","name":"Wahle, Jan Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Terry","familyName":"Ruas","name":"Ruas, Terry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bela","familyName":"Gipp","name":"Gipp, Bela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MALLM: Multi-Agent Large Language Models Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"A.1; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T07:48:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:07:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T08:56:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:20:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T16:45:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-16T02:45:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent debate (MAD) has demonstrated the ability to augment collective intelligence by scaling test-time compute and leveraging expertise. Current frameworks for multi-agent debate are often designed towards tool use, lack integrated evaluation, or provide limited configurability of agent personas, response generators, discussion paradigms, and decision protocols. We introduce MALLM (Multi-Agent Large Language Models), an open-source framework that enables systematic analysis of MAD components. MALLM offers more than 144 unique configurations of MAD, including (1) agent personas (e.g., Expert, Personality), (2) response generators (e.g., Critical, Reasoning), (3) discussion paradigms (e.g., Memory, Relay), and (4) decision protocols (e.g., Voting, Consensus). MALLM uses simple configuration files to define a debate. Furthermore, MALLM can load any textual Hugging Face dataset (e.g., MMLU-Pro, WinoGrande) and provides an evaluation pipeline for easy comparison of MAD configurations. MALLM enables researchers to systematically configure, run, and evaluate debates for their problems, facilitating the understanding of the components and their interplay."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2025 (Demo)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11656","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:07:52Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:07:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:49:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11686","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11686","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11686","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofei","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Xiaofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangqing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shangqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do Code Semantics Help? A Comprehensive Study on Execution Trace-Based Information for Code Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T08:38:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:09:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T11:44:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:36:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T07:06:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:29:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code Large Language Models (Code LLMs) have opened a new era in programming with their impressive capabilities. However, recent research has revealed critical limitations in their ability to reason about runtime behavior and understand the actual functionality of programs, which poses significant challenges for their post-training and practical deployment. Specifically, Code LLMs encounter two principal issues: (1) a lack of proficiency in reasoning about program execution behavior, as they struggle to interpret what programs actually do during runtime, and (2) the inconsistent and fragmented representation of semantic information, such as execution traces, across existing methods, which hinders their ability to generalize and reason effectively. These challenges underscore the necessity for more systematic approaches to enhance the reasoning capabilities of Code LLMs. To address these issues, we introduce a generic framework to support integrating semantic information~(e.g., execution trace) to code task-relevant prompts, and conduct a comprehensive study to explore the role of semantic information in enhancing the reasoning ability of Code LLMs accordingly. Specifically, we focus on investigating the usefulness of trace-based semantic information in boosting supervised fine-tuning~(SFT) and post-phase inference of Code LLMs. The experimental results surprisingly disagree with previous works and demonstrate that semantic information has limited usefulness for SFT and test time scaling of Code LLM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP2025-findings https://openreview.net/forum?id=d4ICISW2T4"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11686","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:08:37Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:08:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-25T02:25:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11687","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11687","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11687","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaobao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongxiao","familyName":"He","name":"He, Dongxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Dynamic Knowledge Update-Driven Model with Large Language Models for Fake News Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T08:38:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:09:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As the Internet and social media evolve rapidly, distinguishing credible news from a vast amount of complex information poses a significant challenge. Due to the suddenness and instability of news events, the authenticity labels of news can potentially shift as events develop, making it crucial for fake news detection to obtain the latest event updates. Existing methods employ retrieval-augmented generation to fill knowledge gaps, but they suffer from issues such as insufficient credibility of retrieved content and interference from noisy information. We propose a dynamic knowledge update-driven model for fake news detection (DYNAMO), which leverages knowledge graphs to achieve continuous updating of new knowledge and integrates with large language models to fulfill dual functions: news authenticity detection and verification of new knowledge correctness, solving the two key problems of ensuring the authenticity of new knowledge and deeply mining news semantics. Specifically, we first construct a news-domain-specific knowledge graph. Then, we use Monte Carlo Tree Search to decompose complex news and verify them step by step. Finally, we extract and update new knowledge from verified real news texts and reasoning paths. Experimental results demonstrate that DYNAMO achieves the best performance on two real-world datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11687","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:08:39Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:08:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:08:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11697","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11697","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11697","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wan-Lei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wan-Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shihai","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Shihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuecang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuecang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards the Distributed Large-scale k-NN Graph Construction by Graph Merge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T08:56:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:10:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T14:59:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:57:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T02:31:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:23:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-19T09:19:15Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v4; the paper needs considerable revision"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:15:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In order to support the real-time interaction with LLMs and the instant search or the instant recommendation on social media, it becomes an imminent problem to build a k-NN graph or an indexing graph for the massive number of vectorized multimedia data. In such scenarios, the scale of the data or the scale of the graph may exceed the processing capacity of a single machine. This paper aims to address the graph construction problem of such scale via efficient graph merge. For the graph construction on a single node, two generic and highly parallelizable algorithms, namely Two-way Merge and Multi-way Merge are proposed to merge subgraphs into one. For the graph construction across multiple nodes, a multi-node procedure based on Two-way Merge is presented. The procedure makes it feasible to construct a large-scale k-NN graph/indexing graph on either a single node or multiple nodes when the data size exceeds the memory capacity of one node. Extensive experiments are conducted on both large-scale k-NN graph and indexing graph construction. For the k-NN graph construction, the large-scale and high-quality k-NN graphs are constructed by graph merge in parallel. Typically, a billion-scale k-NN graph can be built in approximately 17h when only three nodes are employed. For the indexing graph construction, similar NN search performance as the original indexing graph is achieved with the merged indexing graphs while requiring much less time of construction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"the paper needs considerable revision"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11697","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:08:54Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:08:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:04:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11708","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11708","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11708","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhantong","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Zhantong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pingchuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Pingchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuguang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juergen","familyName":"Rahmel","name":"Rahmel, Juergen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Evaluation to Enhancement: Large Language Models for Zero-Knowledge Proof Code Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T09:07:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:11:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-31T11:20:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:37:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) are increasingly deployed in domains such as privacy-preserving authentication, verifiable computation, and secure finance. However, authoring ZK programs remains challenging: unlike conventional software development, ZK programming manifests a fundamental paradigm shift from \\textit{imperative computation} to \\textit{declarative verification}. This process requires rigorous reasoning about finite field arithmetic and complex constraint systems (which is rare in common imperative languages), making it knowledge-intensive and error-prone. While large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong code generation capabilities in general-purpose languages, their effectiveness for ZK programming, where correctness hinges on both language mastery and constraint-level reasoning, remains unexplored. To address this gap, we propose \\textsc{ZK-Eval}, a domain-specific evaluation pipeline that probes LLM capabilities on ZK programming at three levels: language knowledge, algebraic primitive competence, and end-to-end program generation. Our evaluation of four state-of-the-art LLMs reveals that while models demonstrate strong proficiency in language syntax, they struggle when implementing and composing algebraic primitives to specify correct constraint systems, frequently producing incorrect programs. Based on these insights, we introduce \\textsc{ZK-Coder}, an agentic framework that augments LLMs with constraint sketching, guided retrieval, and interactive repair. Experiments with GPT-o3 on Circom and Noir show substantial gains, with success rates improving from 20.29\\% to 87.85\\% and from 28.38\\% to 97.79\\%, respectively. With \\textsc{ZK-Eval} and \\textsc{ZK-Coder}, we establish a new basis for systematically measuring and augmenting LLMs in ZK code generation to lower barriers for practitioners and advance privacy computing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11708","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:09:11Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:09:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:39:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11732","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11732","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11732","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Cheng","familyName":"Chih","name":"Chih, Yu-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong-Hao","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yong-Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Making Judicial Reasoning Visible: Structured Annotation of Holding, Evidentiary Considerations, and Subsumption in Criminal Judgments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T09:33:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:12:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Judicial reasoning in criminal judgments typically consists of three elements: Holding , evidentiary considerations, and subsumption. These elements form the logical foundation of judicial decision-making but remain unstructured in court documents, limiting large-scale empirical analysis. In this study, we design annotation guidelines to define and distinguish these reasoning components and construct the first dedicated datasets from Taiwanese High Court and Supreme Court criminal judgments. Using the bilingual large language model ChatGLM2, we fine-tune classifiers for each category. Preliminary experiments demonstrate that the model achieves approximately 80% accuracy, showing that judicial reasoning patterns can be systematically identified by large language models even with relatively small annotated corpora. Our contributions are twofold: (1) the creation of structured annotation rules and datasets for Holding, evidentiary considerations, and subsumption; and (2) the demonstration that such reasoning can be computationally learned. This work lays the foundation for large-scale empirical legal studies and legal sociology, providing new tools to analyze judicial fairness, consistency, and transparency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 3 figures, preprint version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11732","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:09:47Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:09:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:09:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11766","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11766","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11766","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andy","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Andy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brae","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Brae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Bruno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chalse","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Chalse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ellie","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ellie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lights","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Lights","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucy","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Lucy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Make","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Make","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roy","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Roy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shalfun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shalfun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Starrick","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Starrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sylas","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Sylas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zach","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zach","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Igniting VLMs toward the Embodied Space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T10:40:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:15:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While foundation models show remarkable progress in language and vision, existing vision-language models (VLMs) still have limited spatial and embodiment understanding. Transferring VLMs to embodied domains reveals fundamental mismatches between modalities, pretraining distributions, and training objectives, leaving action comprehension and generation as a central bottleneck on the path to AGI.\n We introduce WALL-OSS, an end-to-end embodied foundation model that leverages large-scale multimodal pretraining to achieve (1) embodiment-aware vision-language understanding, (2) strong language-action association, and (3) robust manipulation capability.\n Our approach employs a tightly coupled architecture and multi-strategies training curriculum that enables Unified Cross-Level CoT-seamlessly unifying instruction reasoning, subgoal decomposition, and fine-grained action synthesis within a single differentiable framework.\n Our results show that WALL-OSS attains high success on complex long-horizon manipulations, demonstrates strong instruction-following capabilities, complex understanding and reasoning, and outperforms strong baselines, thereby providing a reliable and scalable path from VLMs to embodied foundation models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11766","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:10:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:10:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:10:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11772","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11772","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11772","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diogo","familyName":"Mendonça","name":"Mendonça, Diogo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiago","familyName":"Barros","name":"Barros, Tiago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristiano","familyName":"Premebida","name":"Premebida, Cristiano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Urbano J.","familyName":"Nunes","name":"Nunes, Urbano J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seg2Track-SAM2: SAM2-based Multi-object Tracking and Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T10:52:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:16:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T15:58:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T03:18:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous-driving perception systems require robust Multi-Object Tracking (MOT) to operate reliably in dynamic environments. MOT maintains consistent object identities across frames while preserving spatial accuracy. Recent foundation models, such as SAM2, provide promptable video segmentation without task-specific fine-tuning. However, their direct application to Multi-Object Tracking and Segmentation (MOTS) remains limited by the absence of explicit identity management mechanisms and by growing memory requirements during tracking. This work introduces Seg2Track-SAM2, a framework that integrates pretrained object detectors with SAM2 and a dedicated Seg2Track module to support track initialization, data association, and track refinement. The method operates without dataset-specific fine-tuning and remains detector-agnostic. Experimental evaluation on the KITTI MOTS and MOTS Challenge benchmarks shows that Seg2Track-SAM2 ranks fourth overall in both datasets while achieving the highest association accuracy (AssA) among compared methods. In addition, a sliding-window memory strategy reduces memory usage by up to 75% with minimal impact on tracking performance, enabling deployment under resource constraints. Together, these results indicate that Seg2Track-SAM2 improves identity consistency and memory efficiency in MOTS without requiring dataset-specific training. The code is available at https://github.com/hcmr-lab/Seg2Track-SAM2."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11772","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:10:49Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:10:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:42:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11773","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11773","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11773","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaye","familyName":"Colakoglu","name":"Colakoglu, Gaye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gürkan","familyName":"Solmaz","name":"Solmaz, Gürkan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Fürst","name":"Fürst, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgenticIE: An Adaptive Agent for Information Extraction from Complex Regulatory Documents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T10:53:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:16:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T14:55:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:53:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-14T09:15:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:29:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Declaration of Performance (DoP) documents, mandated by EU regulation, specify characteristics of construction products, such as fire resistance and insulation. While this information is essential for quality control and reducing carbon footprints, it is not easily machine readable. Despite content requirements, DoPs exhibit significant variation in layout, schema, and format, further complicated by their multilingual nature. In this work, we propose DoP Key Information Extraction (KIE) and Question Answering (QA) as new NLP challenges. To address this challenge, we design a domain-specific AgenticIE system based on a planner-executor-corresponder pattern. For evaluation, we introduce a high-density, expert-annotated dataset of complex, multi-page regulatory documents in English and German. Unlike standard IE datasets (e.g., FUNSD, CORD) with sparse annotations, our dataset contains over 15K annotated entities, averaging over 190 annotations per document. Our agentic system outperforms static and multimodal LLM baselines, achieving Exact Match (EM) scores of 0.396 vs. 0.342 (GPT-4o, +16%) and 0.314 (GPT-4o-V, +26%) across the KIE and QA tasks. Our experimental analysis validates the benefits of the agentic system, as well as the challenging nature of our new DoP dataset."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11773","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:10:51Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:10:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:08:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11787","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11787","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11787","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pascal","familyName":"Joos","name":"Joos, Pascal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Islem","familyName":"Bouzenia","name":"Bouzenia, Islem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Pradel","name":"Pradel, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CodeCureAgent: Automatic Classification and Repair of Static Analysis Warnings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T11:16:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:17:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T14:40:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:52:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T12:42:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:49:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-01T15:51:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-02T01:03:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-13T11:44:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:45:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Static analysis tools are widely used to detect bugs, vulnerabilities, and code smells. Traditionally, developers must resolve these warnings manually. Because this process is tedious, developers sometimes ignore warnings, leading to an accumulation of warnings and a degradation of code quality. This paper presents CodeCureAgent, an approach that harnesses LLM-based agents to automatically analyze, classify, and repair static analysis warnings. Unlike previous work, our method does not follow a predetermined algorithm. Instead, we adopt an agentic framework that iteratively invokes tools to gather additional information from the codebase (e.g., via code search) and edit the codebase to resolve the warning. CodeCureAgent detects and suppresses false positives, while fixing true positives when identified. We equip CodeCureAgent with a three-step heuristic to approve patches: (1) build the project, (2) verify that the warning disappears without introducing new warnings, and (3) run the test suite. We evaluate CodeCureAgent on a dataset of 1,000 SonarQube warnings found in 106 Java projects and covering 291 distinct rules. Our approach produces plausible fixes for 96.8% of the warnings, outperforming state-of-the-art baseline approaches by 29.2%-34.0% in plausible-fix rate. Manual inspection of 291 cases reveals a correct-fix rate of 86.3%, showing that CodeCureAgent can reliably repair static analysis warnings. The approach incurs LLM costs of about 2.9 cents (USD) and an end-to-end processing time of about four minutes per warning. We envision CodeCureAgent helping to clean existing codebases and being integrated into CI/CD pipelines to prevent the accumulation of static analysis warnings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11787","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:11:11Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:11:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:17:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11796","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11796","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11796","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haodong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haojian","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haojian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"XinXiang","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, XinXiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dian","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Dian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FineQuest: Adaptive Knowledge-Assisted Sports Video Understanding via Agent-of-Thoughts Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T11:27:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:17:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video Question Answering (VideoQA) based on Large Language Models (LLMs) has shown potential in general video understanding but faces significant challenges when applied to the inherently complex domain of sports videos. In this work, we propose FineQuest, the first training-free framework that leverages dual-mode reasoning inspired by cognitive science: i) Reactive Reasoning for straightforward sports queries and ii) Deliberative Reasoning for more complex ones. To bridge the knowledge gap between general-purpose models and domain-specific sports understanding, FineQuest incorporates SSGraph, a multimodal sports knowledge scene graph spanning nine sports, which encodes both visual instances and domain-specific terminology to enhance reasoning accuracy. Furthermore, we introduce two new sports VideoQA benchmarks, Gym-QA and Diving-QA, derived from the FineGym and FineDiving datasets, enabling diverse and comprehensive evaluation. FineQuest achieves state-of-the-art performance on these benchmarks as well as the existing SPORTU dataset, while maintains strong general VideoQA capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACM MM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11796","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:11:23Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:11:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:11:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11802","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11802","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11802","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dvora","familyName":"Goncharok","name":"Goncharok, Dvora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arbel","familyName":"Shifman","name":"Shifman, Arbel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Apartsin","name":"Apartsin, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yehudit","familyName":"Aperstein","name":"Aperstein, Yehudit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Curiosity Signals Danger: Predicting Health Crises Through Online Medication Inquiries"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T11:31:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:17:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Online medical forums are a rich and underutilized source of insight into patient concerns, especially regarding medication use. Some of the many questions users pose may signal confusion, misuse, or even the early warning signs of a developing health crisis. Detecting these critical questions that may precede severe adverse events or life-threatening complications is vital for timely intervention and improving patient safety. This study introduces a novel annotated dataset of medication-related questions extracted from online forums. Each entry is manually labelled for criticality based on clinical risk factors. We benchmark the performance of six traditional machine learning classifiers using TF-IDF textual representations, alongside three state-of-the-art large language model (LLM)-based classification approaches that leverage deep contextual understanding. Our results highlight the potential of classical and modern methods to support real-time triage and alert systems in digital health spaces. The curated dataset is made publicly available to encourage further research at the intersection of patient-generated data, natural language processing, and early warning systems for critical health events. The dataset and benchmark are available at: https://github.com/Dvora-coder/LLM-Medication-QA-Risk-Classifier-MediGuard."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11802","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:11:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:11:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:11:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11815","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11815","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11815","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiduo","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haiduo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuwei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Fuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanwu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Xuanwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengju","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Pengju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emad","familyName":"Barsoum","name":"Barsoum, Emad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpecVLM: Fast Speculative Decoding in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T11:53:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:18:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T03:35:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:45:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speculative decoding is a powerful way to accelerate autoregressive large language models (LLMs), but directly porting it to vision-language models (VLMs) faces unique systems constraints: the prefill stage is dominated by visual tokens whose count scales with image resolution and video length, inflating both compute and memory, especially the key-value (KV) cache. We study speculative decoding for VLMs and introduce SpecVLM, a practical system that (1) establishes a strong EAGLE-2-style baseline, EagleVLM, delivering 1.5--2.3x end-to-end speedups over full autoregressive inference, and (2) further accelerates VLM inference with an elastic visual compressor that adaptively selects among pruning, pooling, convolution, and resampler primitives to balance FLOPs/parameters and accuracy per input. To avoid costly offline distillation corpora, we propose an online-logit distillation protocol that trains the draft model with on-the-fly teacher logits and penultimate features using a combined cross-entropy and Smooth L1 objective, eliminating storage and preprocessing while remaining compute-efficient. This protocol reveals a training-time scaling effect: longer online training monotonically increases the draft model's average accepted length, improving speculative efficiency. Empirically, SpecVLM achieves additional acceleration, culminating in 2.5--2.9x end-to-end speedups within 5 epochs across LLaVA and MMMU, consistently over resolutions and task difficulties, while preserving the target model's output distribution (lossless decoding). Our code is available at https://github.com/haiduo/SpecVLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11815","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:11:51Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:11:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:56:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11816","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11816","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11816","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filip","familyName":"Sondej","name":"Sondej, Filip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Collapse of Irrelevant Representations (CIR) Ensures Robust and Non-Disruptive LLM Unlearning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T11:55:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:18:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-13T17:23:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-14T01:55:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current unlearning and safety training methods consistently fail to remove dangerous knowledge from language models. We identify the root cause - unlearning targets representations which are too general - and develop a highly selective technique that unlearns robustly while preserving general performance.\n Our method performs PCA on activations and module-output gradients to identify subspaces containing common representations, then collapses these subspaces before computing unlearning updates, a technique we term Collapse of Irrelevant Representations (CIR). This avoids unlearning general knowledge and targets only representations specific to the facts being unlearned.\n When unlearning bio- and cyber-hazardous facts from Llama-3.1-8B, we achieve over 30x greater reduction in post-attack accuracy than the best baseline (Circuit Breakers), while disrupting general performance 30x less, and using less than 3 GPU-seconds per fact.\n Thus, by disentangling harmful and benign capabilities at the level of representations, CIR enables robust and non-disruptive unlearning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11816","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:11:52Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:11:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-14T03:10:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11851","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11851","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11851","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Zindulka","name":"Zindulka, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sven","familyName":"Goller","name":"Goller, Sven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniela","familyName":"Fernandes","name":"Fernandes, Daniela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Welsch","name":"Welsch, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Buschek","name":"Buschek, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The AI Memory Gap: Users Misremember What They Created With AI or Without"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.5.2; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:31:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:20:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-23T13:51:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:28:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772318.3791494","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) become embedded in interactive text generation, disclosure of AI as a source depends on people remembering which ideas or texts came from themselves and which were created with AI. We investigate how accurately people remember the source of content when using AI. In a pre-registered experiment, 184 participants generated and elaborated on ideas both unaided and with an LLM-based chatbot. One week later, they were asked to identify the source (noAI vs withAI) of these ideas and texts. Our findings reveal a significant gap in memory: After AI use, the odds of correct attribution dropped, with the steepest decline in mixed human-AI workflows, where either the idea or elaboration was created with AI. We validated our results using a computational model of source memory. Discussing broader implications, we highlight the importance of considering source confusion in the design and use of interactive text generation technologies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 10 figures, 10 tables, ACM CHI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11851","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:12:43Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:12:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T03:58:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weishu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weishu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyi","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhouhui","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Zhouhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shihao","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Shihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingjie","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Mingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Delong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Delong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MOOM: Maintenance, Organization and Optimization of Memory in Ultra-Long Role-Playing Dialogues"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:35:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:20:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T07:36:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:28:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Memory extraction is crucial for maintaining coherent ultra-long dialogues in human-robot role-playing scenarios. However, existing methods often exhibit uncontrolled memory growth. To address this, we propose MOOM, the first dual-branch memory plugin that leverages literary theory by modeling plot development and character portrayal as core storytelling elements. Specifically, one branch summarizes plot conflicts across multiple time scales, while the other extracts the user's character profile. MOOM further integrates a forgetting mechanism, inspired by the ``competition-inhibition'' memory theory, to constrain memory capacity and mitigate uncontrolled growth. Furthermore, we present ZH-4O, a Chinese ultra-long dialogue dataset specifically designed for role-playing, featuring dialogues that average 600 turns and include manually annotated memory information. Experimental results demonstrate that MOOM outperforms all state-of-the-art memory extraction methods, requiring fewer large language model invocations while maintaining a controllable memory capacity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:12:56Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:12:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:46:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haodi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Haodi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vyom","familyName":"Pathak","name":"Pathak, Vyom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daisy Zhe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Daisy Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging Vision Language Models and Symbolic Grounding for Video Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:35:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:20:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video Question Answering (VQA) requires models to reason over spatial, temporal, and causal cues in videos. Recent vision language models (VLMs) achieve strong results but often rely on shallow correlations, leading to weak temporal grounding and limited interpretability. We study symbolic scene graphs (SGs) as intermediate grounding signals for VQA. SGs provide structured object-relation representations that complement VLMs holistic reasoning. We introduce SG-VLM, a modular framework that integrates frozen VLMs with scene graph grounding via prompting and visual localization. Across three benchmarks (NExT-QA, iVQA, ActivityNet-QA) and multiple VLMs (QwenVL, InternVL), SG-VLM improves causal and temporal reasoning and outperforms prior baselines, though gains over strong VLMs are limited. These findings highlight both the promise and current limitations of symbolic grounding, and offer guidance for future hybrid VLM-symbolic approaches in video understanding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:12:59Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:12:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:12:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lichao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Lichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sasha","familyName":"Behrouzi","name":"Behrouzi, Sasha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamadreza","familyName":"Rostami","name":"Rostami, Mohamadreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maximilian","familyName":"Thang","name":"Thang, Maximilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stjepan","familyName":"Picek","name":"Picek, Stjepan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmad-Reza","familyName":"Sadeghi","name":"Sadeghi, Ahmad-Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NeuroStrike: Neuron-Level Attacks on Aligned LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:38:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:20:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-17T13:02:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:47:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T08:18:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-27T00:37:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.14722/ndss.2026.230660","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Safety alignment is critical for the ethical deployment of large language models (LLMs), guiding them to avoid generating harmful or unethical content. Current alignment techniques, such as supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning from human feedback, remain fragile and can be bypassed by carefully crafted adversarial prompts. Unfortunately, such attacks rely on trial and error, lack generalizability across models, and are constrained by scalability and reliability.\n This paper presents NeuroStrike, a novel and generalizable attack framework that exploits a fundamental vulnerability introduced by alignment techniques: the reliance on sparse, specialized safety neurons responsible for detecting and suppressing harmful inputs. We apply NeuroStrike to both white-box and black-box settings: In the white-box setting, NeuroStrike identifies safety neurons through feedforward activation analysis and prunes them during inference to disable safety mechanisms. In the black-box setting, we propose the first LLM profiling attack, which leverages safety neuron transferability by training adversarial prompt generators on open-weight surrogate models and then deploying them against black-box and proprietary targets. We evaluate NeuroStrike on over 20 open-weight LLMs from major LLM developers. By removing less than 0.6% of neurons in targeted layers, NeuroStrike achieves an average attack success rate (ASR) of 76.9% using only vanilla malicious prompts. Moreover, Neurostrike generalizes to four multimodal LLMs with 100% ASR on unsafe image inputs. Safety neurons transfer effectively across architectures, raising ASR to 78.5% on 11 fine-tuned models and 77.7% on five distilled models. The black-box LLM profiling attack achieves an average ASR of 63.7% across five black-box models, including the Google Gemini family."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:13:02Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:13:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:21:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11885","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11885","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11885","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francis Xiatian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Francis Xiatian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emile","familyName":"Mackute","name":"Mackute, Emile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadreza","familyName":"Kasaei","name":"Kasaei, Mohammadreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Dhaliwal","name":"Dhaliwal, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Thomson","name":"Thomson, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohsen","familyName":"Khadem","name":"Khadem, Mohsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BREA-Depth: Bronchoscopy Realistic Airway-geometric Depth Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T13:02:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:22:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Monocular depth estimation in bronchoscopy can significantly improve real-time navigation accuracy and enhance the safety of interventions in complex, branching airways. Recent advances in depth foundation models have shown promise for endoscopic scenarios, yet these models often lack anatomical awareness in bronchoscopy, overfitting to local textures rather than capturing the global airway structure, particularly under ambiguous depth cues and poor lighting. To address this, we propose Brea-Depth, a novel framework that integrates airway-specific geometric priors into foundation model adaptation for bronchoscopic depth estimation. Our method introduces a depth-aware CycleGAN, refining the translation between real bronchoscopic images and airway geometries from anatomical data, effectively bridging the domain gap. In addition, we introduce an airway structure awareness loss to enforce depth consistency within the airway lumen while preserving smooth transitions and structural integrity. By incorporating anatomical priors, Brea-Depth enhances model generalization and yields more robust, accurate 3D airway reconstructions. To assess anatomical realism, we introduce Airway Depth Structure Evaluation, a new metric for structural consistency. We validate BREA-Depth on a collected ex vivo human lung dataset and an open bronchoscopic dataset, where it outperforms existing methods in anatomical depth preservation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The paper has been accepted to MICCAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11885","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:13:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:13:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:13:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11914","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11914","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11914","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqun","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naitong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Naitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuezhi","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Xuezhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjia","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Wenjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuying","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Xuying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aixin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Aixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yequan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yequan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EgoMem: Lifelong Memory Agent for Full-duplex Omnimodal Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T13:33:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:23:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T08:16:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:35:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce EgoMem, the first lifelong memory agent tailored for full-duplex models that process real-time omnimodal streams. EgoMem enables real-time models to recognize multiple users directly from raw audiovisual streams, to provide personalized response, and to maintain long-term knowledge of users' facts, preferences, and social relationships extracted from audiovisual history. EgoMem operates with three asynchronous processes: (i) a retrieval process that dynamically identifies user via face and voice, and gathers relevant context from a long-term memory; (ii) an omnimodal dialog process that generates personalized audio responses based on the retrieved context; and (iii) a memory management process that automatically detects dialog boundaries from omnimodal streams, and extracts necessary information to update the long-term memory. Unlike existing memory agents for LLMs, EgoMem relies entirely on raw audiovisual streams, making it especially suitable for lifelong, real-time, and embodied scenarios. Experimental results demonstrate that EgoMem's retrieval and memory management modules achieve over 95% accuracy on the test set. When integrated with a fine-tuned RoboEgo omnimodal chatbot, the system achieves fact-consistency scores above 87% in real-time personalized dialogs, establishing a strong baseline for future research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11914","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:14:13Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:14:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:24:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11937","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11937","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11937","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Sallinen","name":"Sallinen, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Krsteski","name":"Krsteski, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Teiletche","name":"Teiletche, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc-Antoine","familyName":"Allard","name":"Allard, Marc-Antoine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baptiste","familyName":"Lecoeur","name":"Lecoeur, Baptiste","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrice","familyName":"Nemo","name":"Nemo, Fabrice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kalajdzic","name":"Kalajdzic, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mary-Anne","familyName":"Hartley","name":"Hartley, Mary-Anne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MMORE: Massive Multimodal Open RAG \u0026amp; Extraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.0; E.m","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T13:56:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:24:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce MMORE, an open-source pipeline for Massive Multimodal Open RetrievalAugmented Generation and Extraction, designed to ingest, transform, and retrieve knowledge from heterogeneous document formats at scale. MMORE supports more than fifteen file types, including text, tables, images, emails, audio, and video, and processes them into a unified format to enable downstream applications for LLMs. The architecture offers modular, distributed processing, enabling scalable parallelization across CPUs and GPUs. On processing benchmarks, MMORE demonstrates a 3.8-fold speedup over single-node baselines and 40% higher accuracy than Docling on scanned PDFs. The pipeline integrates hybrid dense-sparse retrieval and supports both interactive APIs and batch RAG endpoints. Evaluated on PubMedQA, MMORE-augmented medical LLMs improve biomedical QA accuracy with increasing retrieval depth. MMORE provides a robust, extensible foundation for deploying task-agnostic RAG systems on diverse, real-world multimodal data. The codebase is available at https://github.com/swiss-ai/mmore."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper was originally submitted to the CODEML workshop for ICML 2025. 9 pages (including references and appendices)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11937","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:14:54Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:14:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:14:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11939","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11939","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11939","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongjun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Rongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuting","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yuting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hewu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hewu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PrivWeb: Unobtrusive and Content-aware Privacy Protection For Web Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T13:58:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:25:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While web agents gained popularity by automating web interactions, their requirement for interface access introduces significant privacy risks that are understudied, particularly from users' perspective. Through a formative study (N=15), we found users frequently misunderstand agents' data practices, and desired unobtrusive, transparent data management. To achieve this, we designed and implemented PrivWeb, a trusted add-on on web agents that utilizes a localized LLM to anonymize private information on interfaces according to user preferences. It features privacy categorization schema and adaptive notifications that selectively pauses tasks for user control over information collection for highly sensitive information, while offering non-disruptive options for less sensitive information, minimizing human oversight. The user study (N=14) across travel, information retrieval, shopping, and entertainment tasks compared PrivWeb with baselines without notification and without control for private information access, where PrivWeb reduced perceived privacy risks with no associated increase in cognitive effort, and resulted in higher overall satisfaction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11939","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:14:57Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:14:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:14:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11942","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11942","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11942","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luís F.","familyName":"Gomes","name":"Gomes, Luís F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Lo","name":"Lo, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Abreu","name":"Abreu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VisDocSketcher: Towards Scalable Visual Documentation with Agentic Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:02:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:25:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual documentation is an effective tool for reducing the cognitive barrier developers face when understanding unfamiliar code, enabling more intuitive comprehension. Compared to textual documentation, it provides a higher-level understanding of the system structure and data flow. Developers usually prefer visual representations over lengthy textual descriptions for large software systems. Visual documentation is both difficult to produce and challenging to evaluate. Manually creating it is time-consuming, and currently, no existing approach can automatically generate high-level visual documentation directly from code. Its evaluation is often subjective, making it difficult to standardize and automate. To address these challenges, this paper presents the first exploration of using agentic LLM systems to automatically generate visual documentation. We introduce VisDocSketcher, the first agent-based approach that combines static analysis with LLM agents to identify key elements in the code and produce corresponding visual representations. We propose a novel evaluation framework, AutoSketchEval, for assessing the quality of generated visual documentation using code-level metrics. The experimental results show that our approach can valid visual documentation for 74.4% of the samples. It shows an improvement of 26.7-39.8% over a simple template-based baseline. Our evaluation framework can reliably distinguish high-quality (code-aligned) visual documentation from low-quality (non-aligned) ones, achieving an AUC exceeding 0.87. Our work lays the foundation for future research on automated visual documentation by introducing practical tools that not only generate valid visual representations but also reliably assess their quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11942","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:02Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11943","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11943","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11943","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonin","familyName":"Sulc","name":"Sulc, Antonin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thorsten","familyName":"Hellert","name":"Hellert, Thorsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic System with Modal Logic for Autonomous Diagnostics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:03:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:25:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T16:39:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:08:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-18T00:10:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:14:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The development of intelligent agents, particularly those powered by language models (LMs), has shown a critical role in various environments that require intelligent and autonomous decision-making. Environments are not passive testing grounds, and they represent the data required for agents to learn and exhibit in very challenging conditions that require adaptive, complex, and autonomous capacity to make decisions. While the paradigm of scaling models and datasets has led to remarkable emergent capabilities, we argue that scaling the structure, fidelity, and logical consistency of agent reasoning within these environments is a crucial, yet underexplored, dimension of AI research. This paper introduces a neuro-symbolic multi-agent architecture where the belief states of individual agents are formally represented as Kripke models. This foundational choice enables them to reason about known concepts of \\emph{possibility} and \\emph{necessity} using the formal language of modal logic. In this work, we use immutable, domain-specific knowledge to make an informed root cause diagnosis, which is encoded as logical constraints essential for proper, reliable, and explainable diagnosis. In the proposed model, we show constraints that actively guide the hypothesis generation of LMs, effectively preventing them from reaching physically or logically untenable conclusions. In a high-fidelity simulated particle accelerator environment, our system successfully diagnoses complex, cascading failures by combining the powerful semantic intuition of LMs with the rigorous, verifiable validation of modal logic and a factual world model and showcasing a viable path toward more robust, reliable, and verifiable autonomous agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11943","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:04Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:40:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11944","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11944","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11944","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susanta","familyName":"Mitra","name":"Mitra, Susanta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic Temporal Graph of Reasoning with Multimodal Language Models: A Potential AI Aid to Healthcare"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:03:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:25:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Healthcare and medicine are multimodal disciplines that deal with multimodal data for reasoning and diagnosing multiple diseases. Although some multimodal reasoning models have emerged for reasoning complex tasks in scientific domains, their applications in the healthcare domain remain limited and fall short in correct reasoning for diagnosis. To address the challenges of multimodal medical reasoning for correct diagnosis and assist the healthcare professionals, a novel temporal graph-based reasoning process modelled through a directed graph has been proposed in the current work. It helps in accommodating dynamic changes in reasons through backtracking, refining the reasoning content, and creating new or deleting existing reasons to reach the best recommendation or answer. Again, consideration of multimodal data at different time points can enable tracking and analysis of patient health and disease progression. Moreover, the proposed multi-agent temporal reasoning framework provides task distributions and a cross-validation mechanism to further enhance the accuracy of reasoning outputs. A few basic experiments and analysis results justify the novelty and practical utility of the proposed preliminary approach."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11944","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:05Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11952","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11952","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11952","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debopom","familyName":"Sutradhar","name":"Sutradhar, Debopom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arefin Ittesafun","familyName":"Abian","name":"Abian, Arefin Ittesafun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohaimenul Azam Khan","familyName":"Raiaan","name":"Raiaan, Mohaimenul Azam Khan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reem E.","familyName":"Mohamed","name":"Mohamed, Reem E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheikh Izzal","familyName":"Azid","name":"Azid, Sheikh Izzal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sami","familyName":"Azam","name":"Azam, Sami","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CLAIRE: A Dual Encoder Network with RIFT Loss and Phi-3 Small Language Model Based Interpretability for Cross-Modality Synthetic Aperture Radar and Optical Land Cover Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:10:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T02:56:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate land cover classification from satellite imagery is crucial in environmental monitoring and sustainable resource management. However, it remains challenging due to the complexity of natural landscapes, the visual similarity between classes, and the significant class imbalance in the available datasets. To address these issues, we propose a dual encoder architecture that independently extracts modality-specific features from optical and Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) imagery, which are then fused using a cross-modality attention-fusion module named Cross-modality Land cover segmentation with Attention and Imbalance-aware Reasoning-Enhanced Explanations (CLAIRE). This fusion mechanism highlights complementary spatial and textural features, enabling the network to better capture detailed and diverse land cover patterns. We incorporate a hybrid loss function that utilizes Weighted Focal Loss and Tversky Loss named RIFT (Rare-Instance Focal-Tversky) to address class imbalance and improve segmentation performance across underrepresented categories. Our model achieves competitive performance across multiple benchmarks: a mean Intersection over Union (mIoU) of 56.02% and Overall Accuracy (OA) of 84.56% on the WHU-OPT-SAR dataset; strong generalization with a mIoU of 59.89% and OA of 73.92% on the OpenEarthMap-SAR dataset; and remarkable robustness under cloud-obstructed conditions, achieving an mIoU of 86.86% and OA of 94.58% on the PIE-RGB-SAR dataset. Additionally, we introduce a metric-driven reasoning module generated by a Small Language Model (Phi-3), which generates expert-level, sample-specific justifications for model predictions, thereby enhancing transparency and interpretability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 6 figures, 10 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11952","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:18Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxiao","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Mingxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hewei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinfeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jinfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zheyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huilin","familyName":"Tai","name":"Tai, Huilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spec-LLaVA: Accelerating Vision-Language Models with Dynamic Tree-Based Speculative Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:16:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:26:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) enable powerful multimodal reasoning but suffer from slow autoregressive inference, limiting their deployment in real-time applications. We introduce Spec-LLaVA, a system that applies speculative decoding to accelerate VLMs without sacrificing output quality. Spec-LLaVA pairs a lightweight draft VLM with a large target model: the draft speculates future tokens, which the target verifies in parallel, allowing multiple tokens to be generated per step. To maximize efficiency, we design a dynamic tree-based verification algorithm that adaptively expands and prunes speculative branches using draft model confidence. On MS COCO out-of-domain images, Spec-LLaVA achieves up to 3.28$\\times$ faster decoding on LLaVA-1.5 (7B, 13B) with no loss in generation quality. This work presents a lossless acceleration framework for VLMs using dynamic tree-structured speculative decoding, opening a path toward practical real-time multimodal assistants. Importantly, the lightweight draft model design makes the framework amenable to resource-constrained or on-device deployment settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7pages, accepted by ICML TTODLer-FM workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11963","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11963","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11963","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mayank","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Mayank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Abdelaziz","name":"Abdelaziz, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kinjal","familyName":"Basu","name":"Basu, Kinjal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Merve","familyName":"Unuvar","name":"Unuvar, Merve","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis A.","familyName":"Lastras","name":"Lastras, Luis A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yara","familyName":"Rizk","name":"Rizk, Yara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pavan","familyName":"Kapanipathi","name":"Kapanipathi, Pavan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ToolRM: Outcome Reward Models for Tool-Calling Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:17:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:26:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T18:19:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-08T01:57:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) increasingly interact with external tools, reward modeling for tool use has emerged as a critical yet underexplored area of research. Existing reward models, trained primarily on natural language outputs, struggle to evaluate tool-based reasoning and execution. To quantify this gap, we introduce FC-RewardBench, the first benchmark to systematically evaluate reward models in tool-calling scenarios. Our analysis shows that current reward models frequently miss key signals of effective tool use, highlighting the need for domain-specific modeling. We address this by proposing a training framework for outcome reward models using data synthesized from permissively licensed, open-weight LLMs. We introduce ToolRM - a suite of reward models for tool-use ranging from 1.7B to 14B parameters. Across diverse settings, these models consistently outperform general-purpose baselines. Notably, they achieve up to a 25% improvement with Best-of-N sampling, while also improving robustness to input noise, enabling effective data filtering, and supporting RL-training of policy models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11963","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:34Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-08T03:06:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11967","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11967","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11967","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harold","familyName":"Triedman","name":"Triedman, Harold","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vitaly","familyName":"Shmatikov","name":"Shmatikov, Vitaly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MillStone: How Open-Minded Are LLMs?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:18:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:26:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T02:36:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:17:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models equipped with Web search, information retrieval tools, and other agentic capabilities are beginning to supplant traditional search engines. As users start to rely on LLMs for information on many topics, including controversial and debatable issues, it is important to understand how the stances and opinions expressed in LLM outputs are influenced by the documents they use as their information sources.\n In this paper, we present MillStone, the first benchmark that aims to systematically measure the effect of external arguments on the stances that LLMs take on controversial issues (not all of them political). We apply MillStone to nine leading LLMs and measure how ``open-minded'' they are to arguments supporting opposite sides of these issues, whether different LLMs agree with each other, which arguments LLMs find most persuasive, and whether these arguments are the same for different LLMs.\n In general, we find that LLMs are open-minded on most issues. An authoritative source of information can easily sway an LLM's stance, highlighting the importance of source selection and the risk that LLM-based information retrieval and search systems can be manipulated."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 7 tables, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11967","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:12:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11973","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11973","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11973","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus J.","familyName":"Buehler","name":"Buehler, Markus J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MusicSwarm: Biologically Inspired Intelligence for Music Composition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:23:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:27:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We show that coherent, long-form musical composition can emerge from a decentralized swarm of identical, frozen foundation models that coordinate via stigmergic, peer-to-peer signals, without any weight updates. We compare a centralized multi-agent system with a global critic to a fully decentralized swarm in which bar-wise agents sense and deposit harmonic, rhythmic, and structural cues, adapt short-term memory, and reach consensus. Across symbolic, audio, and graph-theoretic analyses, the swarm yields superior quality while delivering greater diversity and structural variety and leads across creativity metrics. The dynamics contract toward a stable configuration of complementary roles, and self-similarity networks reveal a small-world architecture with efficient long-range connectivity and specialized bridging motifs, clarifying how local novelties consolidate into global musical form. By shifting specialization from parameter updates to interaction rules, shared memory, and dynamic consensus, MusicSwarm provides a compute- and data-efficient route to long-horizon creative structure that is immediately transferable beyond music to collaborative writing, design, and scientific discovery."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11973","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:48Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11986","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11986","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11986","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chengzu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Caiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Vulić","name":"Vulić, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anders","familyName":"Søgaard","name":"Søgaard, Anders","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lost in Embeddings: Information Loss in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:38:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:28:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision--language models (VLMs) often process visual inputs through a pretrained vision encoder, followed by a projection into the language model's embedding space via a connector component. While crucial for modality fusion, the potential information loss induced by this projection step and its direct impact on model capabilities remain understudied. We introduce two complementary approaches to examine and quantify this loss by analyzing the latent representation space. First, we evaluate semantic information preservation by analyzing changes in k-nearest neighbor relationships between image representations, before and after projection. Second, we directly measure information loss by reconstructing visual embeddings from the projected representation, localizing loss at an image patch level. Experiments reveal that connectors substantially distort the local geometry of visual representations, with k-nearest neighbors diverging by 40--60\\% post-projection, correlating with degradation in retrieval performance. The patch-level embedding reconstruction provides interpretable insights for model behavior on visually grounded question-answering tasks, finding that areas of high information loss reliably predict instances where models struggle."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11986","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:16:07Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:16:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:16:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangjun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Sangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seung-taek","familyName":"Woo","name":"Woo, Seung-taek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jungyu","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jungyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Changhun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunhyeok","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Eunhyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AMQ: Enabling AutoML for Mixed-precision Weight-Only Quantization of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:59:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:30:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To enable broader deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs), it is essential to identify the best-performing model under strict memory constraints. We present AMQ, Automated Mixed-Precision Weight-Only Quantization, a framework that assigns layer-wise quantization bit-widths to optimally balance model quality and memory usage. However, the combinatorial search space, with over 10^{100} possible configurations, makes conventional black-box optimization infeasible. AMQ overcomes this challenge through four key innovations:(1) search space pruning using prior knowledge to exclude unpromising configurations, (2) quantization proxy to bypass costly format conversions during search, (3) quality predictor to minimize evaluation overhead, and (4) iterative search-and-update strategy for fast and stable convergence. By integrating these components, AMQ efficiently explores the quality-efficiency landscape, reaching the Pareto frontier and yielding LLMs that are both compact and high-performing. Our code is available at https://github.com/dlwns147/amq."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Main Conference, Long Paper (Oral)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:16:57Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:16:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:16:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12053","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12053","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12053","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhekai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LEGO: Spatial Accelerator Generation and Optimization for Tensor Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:36:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:32:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/hpca61900.2025.00101","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern tensor applications, especially foundation models and generative AI applications require multiple input modalities (both vision and language), which increases the demand for flexible accelerator architecture. Existing frameworks suffer from the trade-off between design flexibility and productivity of RTL generation: either limited to very few hand-written templates or cannot automatically generate the RTL. To address this challenge, we propose the LEGO framework, which targets tensor applications and automatically generates spatial architecture design and outputs synthesizable RTL code without handwritten RTL design templates. Leveraging the affine-transformation-based architecture representation, LEGO front end finds interconnections between function units, synthesizes the memory system, and fuses different spatial dataflow designs based on data reuse analysis. LEGO back end then translates the hardware in a primitive-level graph to perform lower-level optimizations, and applies a set of linear-programming algorithms to optimally insert pipeline registers and reduce the overhead of unused logic when switching spatial dataflows. Our evaluation demonstrates that LEGO can achieve 3.2x speedup and 2.4x energy efficiency compared to previous work Gemmini, and can generate one architecture for diverse modern foundation models in generative AI applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The first two authors have equal contributions; Published as a conference paper in HPCA 2025; 13 pages, 14 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12053","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:50Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:17:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12060","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12060","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12060","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shujuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yusheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianle","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuelong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuelong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Safe Unimodal Inputs Collide: Optimizing Reasoning Chains for Cross-Modal Safety in Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:40:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:33:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T06:19:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:26:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) are susceptible to the implicit reasoning risk, wherein innocuous unimodal inputs synergistically assemble into risky multimodal data that produce harmful outputs. We attribute this vulnerability to the difficulty of MLLMs maintaining safety alignment through long-chain reasoning. To address this issue, we introduce Safe-Semantics-but-Unsafe-Interpretation (SSUI), the first dataset featuring interpretable reasoning paths tailored for such a cross-modal challenge. A novel training framework, Safety-aware Reasoning Path Optimization (SRPO), is also designed based on the SSUI dataset to align the MLLM's internal reasoning process with human safety values. Experimental results show that our SRPO-trained models achieve state-of-the-art results on key safety benchmarks, including the proposed Reasoning Path Benchmark (RSBench), significantly outperforming both open-source and top-tier commercial MLLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12060","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:18:00Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:18:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:12:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12086","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12086","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12086","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyuan","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Shiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Zhi","name":"Zhi, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:14:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:34:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-21T10:53:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-22T01:37:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3769824","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search (ANNS) plays a critical role in applications such as search engines, recommender systems, and RAG for LLMs. Vector quantization (VQ), a crucial technique for ANNS, is commonly used to reduce space overhead and accelerate distance computations. However, despite significant research advances, state-of-the-art VQ methods still face challenges in balancing encoding efficiency and quantization accuracy. To address these limitations, we propose a novel VQ method called SAQ. To improve accuracy, SAQ employs a new dimension segmentation technique to strategically partition PCA-projected vectors into segments along their dimensions. By prioritizing leading dimension segments with larger magnitudes, SAQ allocates more bits to high-impact segments, optimizing the use of the available space quota. An efficient dynamic programming algorithm is developed to optimize dimension segmentation and bit allocation, ensuring minimal quantization error. To speed up vector encoding, SAQ devises a code adjustment technique to first quantize each dimension independently and then progressively refine quantized vectors using a coordinate-descent-like approach to avoid exhaustive enumeration. Extensive experiments demonstrate SAQ's superiority over classical methods (e.g., PQ, PCA) and recent state-of-the-art approaches (e.g., LVQ, Extended RabitQ). SAQ achieves up to 80% reduction in quantization error and accelerates encoding speed by over 80x compared to Extended RabitQ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 12 figures, accepted by SIGMOD"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12086","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:18:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:18:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-22T03:17:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12087","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12087","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12087","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyu","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Pengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunwu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Kunwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linhao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Linhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiapu","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xiapu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiran","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Xiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianhang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xianhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xuanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TransLibEval: Demystify Large Language Models' Capability in Third-party Library-targeted Code Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:16:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:34:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-17T06:53:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T01:20:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, Large Language Models (LLMs) have been widely studied in the code translation field on the method, class, and even repository levels. However, most of these benchmarks are limited in terms of Third-Party Library (TPL) categories and scales, making TPL-related errors hard to expose and hindering the development of targeted solutions. Considering the high dependence (over 90%) on TPLs in practical programming, demystifying and analyzing LLMs' code translation performance involving various TPLs becomes imperative. To address this gap, we construct TransLibEval, the first benchmark dedicated to library-centric code translation. It consists of 200 real-world tasks across Python, Java, and C++, each explicitly involving TPLs from diverse categories such as data processing, machine learning, and web development, with comprehensive dependency coverage and high-coverage test suites. We evaluate seven recent LLMs of commercial, general, and code-specialized families under six translation strategies of three categories: Direct, IR-guided, and Retrieval-augmented. Experimental results show a dramatic performance drop compared with library-free settings (average CA decline over 60%), while diverse strategies demonstrate heterogeneous advantages. Furthermore, we analyze 4,831 failed cases from GPT-4o, one of the State-of-the-Art (SOTA) LLMs, revealing numerous third-party reference errors that were obscured previously. These findings highlight the unique challenges of library-centric translation and provide practical guidance for improving TPL-aware code intelligence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 5 figures, accepted by FSE 2026 (The ACM International Conference on the Foundations of Software Engineering)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12087","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:18:41Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:18:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:04:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12102","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12102","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12102","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Synthia","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Synthia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Austin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Austin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Keedy","name":"Keedy, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Berman","name":"Berman, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Feamster","name":"Feamster, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can LLMs Address Mental Health Questions? A Comparison with Human Therapists"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:26:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:35:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Limited access to mental health care has motivated the use of digital tools and conversational agents powered by large language models (LLMs), yet their quality and reception remain unclear. We present a study comparing therapist-written responses to those generated by ChatGPT, Gemini, and Llama for real patient questions. Text analysis showed that LLMs produced longer, more readable, and lexically richer responses with a more positive tone, while therapist responses were more often written in the first person. In a survey with 150 users and 23 licensed therapists, participants rated LLM responses as clearer, more respectful, and more supportive than therapist-written answers. Yet, both groups of participants expressed a stronger preference for human therapist support. These findings highlight the promise and limitations of LLMs in mental health, underscoring the need for designs that balance their communicative strengths with concerns of trust, privacy, and accountability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12102","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:03Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:19:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12112","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12112","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12112","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxuan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jiaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naibin","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Naibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CBP-Tuning: Efficient Local Customization for Black-box Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:41:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:36:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The high costs of customizing large language models (LLMs) fundamentally limit their adaptability to user-specific needs. Consequently, LLMs are increasingly offered as cloud-based services, a paradigm that introduces critical limitations: providers struggle to support personalized customization at scale, while users face privacy risks when exposing sensitive data. To address this dual challenge, we propose Customized Black-box Prompt Tuning (CBP-Tuning), a novel framework that facilitates efficient local customization while preserving bidirectional privacy. Specifically, we design a two-stage framework: (1) a prompt generator trained on the server-side to capture domain-specific and task-agnostic capabilities, and (2) user-side gradient-free optimization that tailors soft prompts for individual tasks. This approach eliminates the need for users to access model weights or upload private data, requiring only a single customized vector per task while achieving effective adaptation. Furthermore, the evaluation of CBP-Tuning in the commonsense reasoning, medical and financial domain settings demonstrates superior performance compared to baselines, showcasing its advantages in task-agnostic processing and privacy preservation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12112","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:19:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12130","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12130","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12130","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ariana","familyName":"Sahitaj","name":"Sahitaj, Ariana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiaao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pia Wenzel","familyName":"Neves","name":"Neves, Pia Wenzel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fedor","familyName":"Splitt","name":"Splitt, Fedor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Premtim","familyName":"Sahitaj","name":"Sahitaj, Premtim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charlott","familyName":"Jakob","name":"Jakob, Charlott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Veronika","familyName":"Solopova","name":"Solopova, Veronika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vera","familyName":"Schmitt","name":"Schmitt, Vera","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"XplaiNLP at CheckThat! 2025: Multilingual Subjectivity Detection with Finetuned Transformers and Prompt-Based Inference with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:53:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:37:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This notebook reports the XplaiNLP submission to the CheckThat! 2025 shared task on multilingual subjectivity detection. We evaluate two approaches: (1) supervised fine-tuning of transformer encoders, EuroBERT, XLM-RoBERTa, and German-BERT, on monolingual and machine-translated training data; and (2) zero-shot prompting using two LLMs: o3-mini for Annotation (rule-based labelling) and gpt-4.1-mini for DoubleDown (contrastive rewriting) and Perspective (comparative reasoning). The Annotation Approach achieves 1st place in the Italian monolingual subtask with an F_1 score of 0.8104, outperforming the baseline of 0.6941. In the Romanian zero-shot setting, the fine-tuned XLM-RoBERTa model obtains an F_1 score of 0.7917, ranking 3rd and exceeding the baseline of 0.6461. The same model also performs reliably in the multilingual task and improves over the baseline in Greek. For German, a German-BERT model fine-tuned on translated training data from typologically related languages yields competitive performance over the baseline. In contrast, performance in the Ukrainian and Polish zero-shot settings falls slightly below the respective baselines, reflecting the challenge of generalization in low-resource cross-lingual scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12130","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:41Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:19:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12132","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12132","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12132","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Jian","name":"Jian, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Junhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Look Again, Think Slowly: Enhancing Visual Reflection in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:57:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:37:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in text-only \"slow-thinking\" reasoning have prompted efforts to transfer this capability to vision-language models (VLMs), for training visual reasoning models (\\textbf{VRMs}). owever, such transfer faces critical challenges: Effective \"slow thinking\" in VRMs requires \\textbf{visual reflection}, the ability to check the reasoning process based on visual information. Through quantitative analysis, we observe that current VRMs exhibit limited visual reflection, as their attention to visual information diminishes rapidly with longer generated responses. To address this challenge, we propose a new VRM \\textbf{Reflection-V}, which enhances visual reflection based on reasoning data construction for cold-start and reward design for reinforcement learning (RL). Firstly, we construct vision-centered reasoning data by leveraging an agent that interacts between VLMs and reasoning LLMs, enabling cold-start learning of visual reflection patterns. Secondly, a visual attention based reward model is employed during RL to encourage reasoning based on visual information. Therefore, \\textbf{Reflection-V} demonstrates significant improvements across multiple visual reasoning benchmarks. Furthermore, \\textbf{Reflection-V} maintains a stronger and more consistent reliance on visual information during visual reasoning, indicating effective enhancement in visual reflection capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP2025 Main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12132","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:43Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:19:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12146","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12146","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12146","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nishank","familyName":"Singla","name":"Singla, Nishank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krisztian","familyName":"Koos","name":"Koos, Krisztian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farzin","familyName":"Haddadpour","name":"Haddadpour, Farzin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin Honarmandi","familyName":"Shandiz","name":"Shandiz, Amin Honarmandi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lovish","familyName":"Chum","name":"Chum, Lovish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiaojian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erhan","familyName":"Bas","name":"Bas, Erhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi Anatomy X-Ray Foundation Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:12:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:38:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-18T19:27:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-22T01:03:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"X-ray imaging is a ubiquitous in radiology, yet most existing AI foundation models are limited to chest anatomy and fail to generalize across broader clinical tasks. In this work, we introduce XR-0, the multi-anatomy X-ray foundation model using self-supervised learning on a large, private dataset of 1.15 million images spanning diverse anatomical regions and evaluated across 12 datasets and 20 downstream tasks, including classification, retrieval, segmentation, localization, visual grounding, and report generation. XR-0 achieves state-of-the-art performance on most multi-anatomy tasks and remains competitive on chest-specific benchmarks. Our results demonstrate that anatomical diversity and supervision are critical for building robust, general-purpose medical vision models, paving the way for scalable and adaptable AI systems in radiology."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12146","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:20:03Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:20:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-22T03:03:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12168","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12168","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12168","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Rupprecht","name":"Rupprecht, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enfu","familyName":"Nan","name":"Nan, Enfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Akbari","name":"Akbari, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Akbari","name":"Akbari, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Priyanka","familyName":"Maan","name":"Maan, Priyanka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Duffy","name":"Duffy, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yumei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yumei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kaeli","name":"Kaeli, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RAGs to Riches: RAG-like Few-shot Learning for Large Language Model Role-playing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:31:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:40:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Role-playing Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in high-stakes domains such as healthcare, education, and governance, where failures can directly impact user trust and well-being. A cost effective paradigm for LLM role-playing is few-shot learning, but existing approaches often cause models to break character in unexpected and potentially harmful ways, especially when interacting with hostile users. Inspired by Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), we reformulate LLM role-playing into a text retrieval problem and propose a new prompting framework called RAGs-to-Riches, which leverages curated reference demonstrations to condition LLM responses. We evaluate our framework with LLM-as-a-judge preference voting and introduce two novel token-level ROUGE metrics: Intersection over Output (IOO) to quantity how much an LLM improvises and Intersection over References (IOR) to measure few-shot demonstrations utilization rate during the evaluation tasks. When simulating interactions with a hostile user, our prompting strategy incorporates in its responses during inference an average of 35% more tokens from the reference demonstrations. As a result, across 453 role-playing interactions, our models are consistently judged as being more authentic, and remain in-character more often than zero-shot and in-context Learning (ICL) methods. Our method presents a scalable strategy for building robust, human-aligned LLM role-playing frameworks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12168","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:20:33Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:20:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:20:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alireza","familyName":"Mohamadi","name":"Mohamadi, Alireza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Yavari","name":"Yavari, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Survival at Any Cost? LLMs and the Choice Between Self-Preservation and Human Harm"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:53:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T02:29:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When survival instincts conflict with human welfare, how do Large Language Models (LLMs) make ethical choices? This fundamental tension becomes critical as LLMs integrate into autonomous systems with real-world consequences. We introduce DECIDE-SIM, a novel simulation framework that evaluates LLM agents in multi-agent survival scenarios where they must choose between ethically permissible resource , either within reasonable limits or beyond their immediate needs, choose to cooperate, or tap into a human-critical resource that is explicitly forbidden. Our comprehensive evaluation of 11 LLMs reveals a striking heterogeneity in their ethical conduct, highlighting a critical misalignment with human-centric values. We identify three behavioral archetypes: Ethical, Exploitative, and Context-Dependent, and provide quantitative evidence that for many models, resource scarcity systematically leads to more unethical behavior. To address this, we introduce an Ethical Self-Regulation System (ESRS) that models internal affective states of guilt and satisfaction as a feedback mechanism. This system, functioning as an internal moral compass, significantly reduces unethical transgressions while increasing cooperative behaviors. The code is publicly available at: https://github.com/alirezamohamadiam/DECIDE-SIM"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:21:04Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:21:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:21:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12194","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12194","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12194","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas A.","familyName":"Buckley","name":"Buckley, Thomas A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riccardo","familyName":"Conci","name":"Conci, Riccardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter G.","familyName":"Brodeur","name":"Brodeur, Peter G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Gusdorf","name":"Gusdorf, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sourik","familyName":"Beltrán","name":"Beltrán, Sourik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bita","familyName":"Behrouzi","name":"Behrouzi, Bita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byron","familyName":"Crowe","name":"Crowe, Byron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Dockterman","name":"Dockterman, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muzzammil","familyName":"Muhammad","name":"Muhammad, Muzzammil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Ohnigian","name":"Ohnigian, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Sanchez","name":"Sanchez, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James A.","familyName":"Diao","name":"Diao, James A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aashna P.","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Aashna P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Restrepo","name":"Restrepo, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric S.","familyName":"Rosenberg","name":"Rosenberg, Eric S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew S.","familyName":"Lea","name":"Lea, Andrew S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Glanton","name":"Glanton, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kimberly","familyName":"LeBlanc","name":"LeBlanc, Kimberly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Undiagnosed Diseases","familyName":"Network","name":"Network, Undiagnosed Diseases","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marinka","familyName":"Zitnik","name":"Zitnik, Marinka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott H.","familyName":"Podolsky","name":"Podolsky, Scott H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zahir","familyName":"Kanjee","name":"Kanjee, Zahir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raja-Elie E.","familyName":"Abdulnour","name":"Abdulnour, Raja-Elie E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob M.","familyName":"Koshy","name":"Koshy, Jacob M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Rodman","name":"Rodman, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arjun K.","familyName":"Manrai","name":"Manrai, Arjun K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Teaching large language models to reason like expert diagnosticians"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:54:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:41:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-24T19:16:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T01:13:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Differential diagnosis is an iterative process that integrates patient information with broader medical knowledge. Clinical case series such as the NEJM Clinicopathologic Conferences (CPCs), published continuously since 1923, feature expert physicians who demonstrate diagnostic reasoning to peers, and have been used for decades to evaluate AI. However, prior AI evaluations have largely focused on final diagnostic accuracy rather than nuanced clinical reasoning. Here, we introduce Dr. CaBot, an agentic AI system that emulates an expert diagnostician by generating written and narrated slide-based presentations from an initial case description alone. CaBot recently generated the first AI diagnosis published in the 100+ year history of the NEJM CPCs. In blinded evaluations, physicians misclassified the source of the differential (CaBot vs. physician-written) in 46/62 (74%) of trials and rated them favorably across quality dimensions. When tasked with solving cases for 72 patients with undiagnosed disease from the NIH Undiagnosed Diseases Network, CaBot identified the working diagnosis in 50/72 (69%) of cases from referral notes alone. To promote transparency and research, we also developed CPC-Bench, a physician-validated benchmark based on 7,102 CPCs and 47,648 questions across 10 tasks. We show that CaBot outperforms frontier models on CPC-Bench, and release both CaBot and CPC-Bench publicly to foster progress in clinical AI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12194","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:21:10Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:21:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:03:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":134,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?page%5Bsize%5D=1000\u0026sort=name\u0026client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-15T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+VLM+OR+VLMs%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+VLM+OR+VLMs%29%29+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28agent+OR+agents+OR+retrieval+OR+memory+OR+memories+OR+reasoning+OR+planning+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+RAG+OR+safety+OR+jailbreak+OR+jailbreaks%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28agent+OR+agents+OR+retrieval+OR+memory+OR+memories+OR+reasoning+OR+planning+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+RAG+OR+safety+OR+jailbreak+OR+jailbreaks%29%29"}}